"use strict";
// Help for the "?" button in the top right of every page (the slide-out panel).
// There is one panel for every screen and every tab, keyed by what the sidebar and the tab strips show
// (app.js: helpTopicKey).  This is plain data, not HTML: the page's security policy forbids injecting
// markup, so app.js builds the panel from these blocks.  A block is
//   "text"                     a paragraph: **bold**, `code` and *italic* are understood
//   ["h", "Heading"]           a sub-heading
//   ["ul", ["item", ...]]      a bullet list (items understand the same markup)
//   ["ol", ["step", ...]]      a numbered list, for steps done in order
//   ["note", "text"]           a highlighted note, for a warning or a thing people trip over
//   ["fields", [["Label", "what it does"], ...]]  a field list; each Label is the field's exact
//                              on-screen label (test: TestHelpCoversEveryField)
//   ["cli", "x --y\nx --z"]    command-line equivalents, shown as code
// Every panel follows the same plan: what the screen is for, what you can do on it, steps for the
// common jobs, the fields, and what is good to know.  Keep a panel's command-line block in step with
// its screen (every GUI function has a command).
const NTPDIR_HELP = {
  topology: {
    title: "Topology",
    body: [
      "This page draws each gateway as a picture: the shared address your clients use, the NTP servers behind it, and the cluster nodes that serve it. It is the main place to build and change a gateway.",
      "Every change you make here is saved and applied at once. There is no Apply button.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["See at a glance whether a gateway, its NTP servers and its nodes are working.", "Create, rename and delete gateways.", "Add, edit, pause and remove NTP servers, anycast addresses and nodes.", "Open graphs for any server, or the gateway as a whole.", "Take a node out of a gateway, or put it back.", "Check that virtual MAC addresses work on your network."]],
      ["h", "The sidebar"],
      ["ul", ["Each gateway is listed under **Topology** in the sidebar. Click one to see its drawing. Right-click it to **Rename** or **Delete** it. A gateway is shown by its address, or by the **Name** you give it (right-click the circle ▸ **Edit**). The name is shared by every node in the cluster.", "**＋ New gateway** adds another gateway. It starts with four public time servers (pool.ntp.org, time.nist.gov, time.google.com and time.cloudflare.com). Right-click a server ▸ **Delete** for any you do not want.", "The **dot** beside a gateway shows how the whole cluster is doing with it, whichever node you picked in the Node menu. **Green**: at least one node serves it. **Amber**: it is served, but in a reduced way. **Dashed grey**: every node has it paused. **Red**: no node serves it. Hover the dot for the count.", "The drawing shows the gateway as the nodes that serve it see it. If the node you picked does not serve this gateway (it is on another subnet, was removed from the gateway, or has it paused), you see the drawing from a node that does, and the circle's tooltip says which. If no node serves it, you see the node you picked."]],
      ["h", "Reading the drawing"],
      ["ul", ["The **circle** is the gateway. It stands for the shared address (the *VIP*) that clients send NTP requests to. Hover it to see how long the cluster has served it without a break, which nodes serve it right now, which node is the gateway controller, and each node's slot. The **time** inside the circle is the clock of the node you are looking at, counted by the second and checked against the node every 15 seconds. Hover it for the date, chrony's stratum and reference, and the clock's offset. The **Time zone** picker at the top left, on the line of the node picker, chooses the zone it is shown in (UTC, or any zone your browser knows). Click it for the whole list, or type part of a name (`phoe`, `kolkata`) to narrow the list. The choice is kept in a cookie in your browser, separately for each gateway (a gateway you have not picked one for yet uses the zone you picked before gateways had their own, or UTC), so each person sees the zone they last picked for the gateway they are looking at.", "The **squares** are the NTP servers this gateway uses as its time sources. Each one shows its state and how fast it answers. Its name is shown under the address (cut with … when it is wider). Hover one for its stratum, offset and root distance. A server that fails the health checks is taken out of chrony's sources.", "The **parallelograms** on the left are the nodes of the cluster (just this node if it is alone). This node comes first and is marked with a star (\\*). The gateway controller has a dot (•). The cluster's primary has a triangle (▴). Their colours: **green** is serving this gateway; **amber** is up but not serving yet, or serving while its own NTP servers are partly down; **dashed grey** is paused; **dashed red** is not answering. A node whose CPU, memory or fullest disk goes over 85% turns solid yellow until it is back at 85% or below (the tooltip shows the numbers).", "The **pills** on the right are the gateway's anycast addresses (see *Anycast addresses* below).", "The pill under them for a **AnyName address**, when the AnyName DNS Director is installed on the same host (see *AnyName addresses* below)."]],
      "Both sides stand four to a column (three when four would put a line to a server through a box), and spread into more columns after that. Drag an anycast pill, or a cluster node, to any slot to change the order. The order of the nodes is saved with the gateway (`node_order`, shared by the cluster). Nodes you have not placed come after the placed ones, by name, and the order does not depend on which node you look through.",
      ["h", "Right-clicking a node"],
      "Right-click one of the parallelograms to work on that node. The menu offers:",
      ["ul", ["**Controller**: this node takes the gateway controller role for every gateway. The node that held it steps down afterwards.", "**Primary**: makes this node the cluster's primary at once, the same as Cluster ▸ Promote. It is greyed out on the current primary.", "**Rename**: changes the node's host name (its operating system name and its `/etc/hosts` entry). Use letters, digits and `-`, with dots allowed. A name is letters, digits and hyphens, in up to four parts separated by dots. Every name in `/etc/hosts` that was the old host name, short or full, becomes the new one, or a `127.0.1.1` line is added. If the host name cannot be set, `/etc/hosts` is put back.", "**Pause** / **Resume**: pausing the primary hands the primary role to the most up-to-date node that is in service.", "**Restart** and **Shut down**: the whole host of that node, after you confirm. You are warned when a gateway would lose its last serving member.", "**Remove**, **Statistics** and **Open**. A node that has been removed from a gateway is not drawn on it. Right-click the gateway ▸ **Add node** to bring it back."]],
      "Hover a node for its address, its Ethernet interfaces' IPv4 and IPv6 global addresses, when it was last seen, how long its daemon has been running (Up), its version, and whether it has the primary's latest settings.",
      ["h", "Colours"],
      ["ul", ["**Green**: working. **Amber**: degraded (some servers down, or the gateway is still starting). **Red**: down, so clients cannot be answered. **Grey**: not known yet. **Dashed**: paused.", "A gateway that has just been started or resumed shows amber “starting” until its NTP servers answer. It only starts serving once they do, so clients never reach a node that cannot answer yet.", "Hover any item for its **uptime**: how long it has been online since it last came back (or how long it has been down) and how many failures it has had, for example “Online for 1m 1s - 0 failures”. Green and yellow count as online. AnyTime keeps this count itself, even with no browser open, but only in memory, so the count (failures included) starts again when AnyTime restarts. Paused and grey items show nothing. `ntpdir --canvas` prints the same."]],
      ["h", "Working with the drawing"],
      ["ul", ["Right-click the **circle**: *Statistics*, *Edit*, *Add NTP server*, *Add anycast address*, *Pause / Resume* ▸ *This node* or *All nodes*, *Delete*. With AnyName on the same host there is also *Add AnyName address*.", "Right-click a **square**: *Edit*, *Statistics*, *Pause / Resume*, *Delete*. The Delete key also deletes the selected item.", "The **mouse wheel** zooms the drawing around the pointer. Double-click the empty drawing to fit it to the window again. **Drag the empty background** to move around.", "**Drag** a server left or right, or an anycast address up or down, to move it. A dashed outline shows where it will land, **Esc** cancels, and the new order is saved when you let go.", "In **Add NTP server**, fill in the IP address and the **Name** is filled in from its reverse DNS record. Or fill in a host name as the Name and the address is looked up. This happens when you leave the field, and only into a field that is empty or still holds an earlier lookup. Once you change the name to anything you like, it is kept."]],
      ["h", "Step by step"],
      ["h", "Add a NTP server"],
      ["ol", ["Right-click the circle and choose **Add NTP server**.", "Type its IP address or host name. The name fills in by itself."]],
      ["h", "Add an anycast address"],
      ["ol", ["Right-click the circle and choose **Add anycast address**.", "Type an IPv4 or IPv6 address, with no prefix length. It may be in any subnet.", "It appears as a pill on the right. Announce it to your routers on Configure ▸ Anycast, or with your own routing daemon."]],
      ["h", "Pause or resume something"],
      ["ol", ["Right-click the gateway, a server, or an anycast address.", "Choose **Pause ▸ This node** (only this node stops; the others carry on) or **Pause ▸ All nodes** (every node follows).", "Resume the same way. A scope is resumed on its own: an item paused on both stays paused until both are resumed."]],
      ["h", "Take a node out of a gateway, or put it back"],
      ["ol", ["Right-click the node (a parallelogram) and choose **Remove**. It gives up the gateway's address and stops answering for it.", "To put it back, right-click the gateway and choose **Add node**, then pick the node by name. It serves again once its NTP servers answer."]],
      ["h", "Check that virtual MAC addresses work"],
      "**Test virtual MACs** checks that replies addressed to a gateway's virtual MAC reach this node. It asks the router (or another neighbour) a question from a throwaway virtual MAC and listens for the answer, and does the same from the real MAC as a control.",
      ["ol", ["Right-click the circle and choose **Test virtual MACs**.", "Read the answer. **Delivered** means virtual MACs work here. **Not delivered** means your network drops frames sent to a virtual MAC (a VMware port group that is not promiscuous, a cloud with one MAC per interface); the dialog then offers **Use real MAC addresses**, which turns that shared option on for every node. **Inconclusive** means nothing answered even the real MAC, so nothing was proved."]],
      "The test runs once by itself when a gateway starts. Its result is in the circle's tooltip, and a “not delivered” turns a healthy gateway amber. It never changes a setting unless you choose to.",
      ["h", "See graphs"],
      ["ol", ["Right-click a server (a square) or the gateway (the circle) and choose **Statistics**.", "Choose **Last hour**, **Last day** or **Last week**. History is kept for 7 days and survives a restart."]],
      ["ul", ["A server's graphs show **round trip, clock offset and loss**.", "The gateway's graphs show **answer time as clients see it, errors, availability and requests per second**.", "A server's graphs are this node's own. The gateway's graphs add up **every reachable node** of the cluster, because each node only answers the clients that reach it. A note beside the time buttons says how many nodes are counted."]],
      ["h", "Fallback servers"],
      "Fallback servers are set in *Edit* (right-click the circle). They are used only while **no** server of the gateway is in service (every one is down or paused). The gateway then answers from them and shows amber, “answering from the fallback servers”. It goes back to its own servers as soon as one answers again. Fallbacks are never probed and always count as up.",
      ["h", "Several subnets"],
      "A node runs a gateway only if its network interface has an address in the gateway's subnet (IPv4, and IPv6 when the node has a global IPv6 address). On a node that does not, the gateway shows grey (“not running here”) and starts by itself once the node is addressed there. The cluster members on that subnet serve it meanwhile.",
      ["h", "When AnyName is installed (AnyName's gateways)"],
      "On a host that also runs the **AnyName DNS Director**, AnyTime does not use gateway and anycast addresses of its own. It shows **one gateway for each gateway of AnyName**, with AnyName's name, and **answers NTP on that gateway's shared and anycast addresses**.",
      ["ul", ["The gateways appear by themselves and follow AnyName: a gateway or address AnyName gains, loses or renames is picked up within seconds. There is nothing to choose.", "They share one list of NTP servers (right-click ▸ *Add NTP server* on any of them). *New gateway*, *Edit*, *Add anycast address*, *Add AnyName address*, renaming, *Pause* and *Delete* are not offered on them and a node cannot be paused while AnyName is installed: AnyName decides where the addresses answer and chrony answers on them either way, so pausing here would stop nothing. Drain it in AnyName.", "Gateways AnyTime had before AnyName was installed are kept in the configuration but do not run, and show grey with the reason. Removing AnyName brings them back and the mirrored gateways stop.", "AnyTime only listens. AnyName adds, removes and announces the addresses, and AnyTime never touches FRR. The circle shows the gateway's shared addresses, the pills its anycast addresses. It is green when this node holds the gateway's addresses and a server answers, amber when only some are held or some servers are down, and grey when this node holds none of them (the nodes that do hold them answer). AnyName holds a shared address on one node of the gateway at a time, so a node without it is not short of anything."]],
      ["h", "AnyName addresses"],
      "This applies only when the **AnyName DNS Director** is installed on the same host. The two programs cannot share BGP, so ntpdir leaves BGP to AnyName there.",
      ["ul", ["Right-click the circle ▸ **Add AnyName address** lists the anycast addresses that AnyName announces (read from its configuration). Pick one to make the gateway **also answer NTP on that address**. It appears as a pill under the anycast pills.", "chrony answers on the address once AnyName holds it on `lo` and announces it. AnyTime never adds it, removes it or withdraws it.", "The pill is **green** while AnyName has the address on `lo` and a server answers. It is **amber** (with the reason) when AnyName holds it but no NTP server answers, because AnyName still announces it and clients would get no answer. It is **grey** while AnyName does not hold it, no longer announces it, or is gone.", "The list is shared with the cluster. Adding an address that AnyName does not announce is refused. An address already saved stays when AnyName is removed, and is answered again when AnyName comes back. An address cannot also be one of the gateway's own anycast addresses, and only one gateway may answer on it.", "Delete the pill (or press Delete) to stop."]],
      ["h", "Anycast addresses"],
      "An anycast address is an extra IPv4 or IPv6 address, from **any subnet**, that every node running the gateway answers on. Clients reach whichever node your routers send them to.",
      ["ul", ["Add one with right-click ▸ **Add anycast address**. It appears as a pill. Right-click the pill to edit or delete it, or press Delete.", "Anycast addresses are not checked against the interface and take no part in the election. Every node running the gateway puts them on its loopback (`lo`) and chrony answers NTP on them.", "Announce them to your routers on **Configure ▸ Anycast** (BGP or OSPF), or use your own routing daemon.", "An address is taken off `lo` (so the route is withdrawn) while no NTP server answers, or the gateway is paused or stopped. For NTP it also needs chrony to be keeping time.", "It is also taken off `lo` while **neither BGP nor OSPF can carry it**: neither is configured or enabled on this node, or no BGP neighbor of its family has an established session and no OSPF interface has a neighbor in state Full (OSPFv2 for IPv4, OSPFv3 for IPv6). One established neighbor is enough. It comes back within seconds once a session is established.", "It is added with a 10-second lifetime that AnyTime keeps renewing, so if AnyTime crashes the kernel removes it within seconds.", "The drawing shows whether each address is announced from this node. Hover a pill for how long the cluster has had the address up without a break (a rolling update does not reset it, a time when no node announces it does).", "When this node manages BGP or OSPF, a pill is **green** when every neighbor has an established session (for OSPF: every enabled interface has a neighbor in state Full), **amber** when some do and some do not, and **red** when none does, when there is no neighbor of that family, or when the protocol is disabled on this node. A neighbor that is disabled or still connecting counts as not established. IPv4 addresses count IPv4 neighbors, and IPv6 addresses count IPv6 neighbors. With both protocols set up the pill shows the better of the two.", "The **same anycast address can be added to several gateways**, which is the usual anycast setup where every site announces one address. It is then held on `lo` while **any** of those gateways has an NTP server answering and chrony keeping time. A gateway that cannot answer shows “kept up by …” in the pill's tooltip.", "An anycast address cannot be the same as a gateway's shared address."]],
      ["h", "Pause and resume: the rules"],
      ["ul", ["**This node** is kept in this node's own settings, so the other nodes carry on. **All nodes** is shared, so every node follows.", "A paused **gateway** releases its address and stops answering. A paused **server** gets no requests or probes. A paused **anycast address** is not announced (the gateway keeps serving).", "The last server in service cannot be paused unless the gateway has fallback servers to answer from. A server that is down or paused is not in service. With every other server down or paused, the page refuses and says why.", "The last node in service cannot be paused either (Pause on the node's menu, Operate ▸ Node, `ntpdir --node-pause`). The other nodes must answer and not be paused, and fallback servers do not change that. Nor can the last node serving a gateway be paused: every gateway the node serves must also be served by another node that is not paused. A gateway can still be paused on all nodes.", "A gateway turns **amber** when only one node is left serving it (the others paused or down), and also when a gateway with three or more servers has only one left in service. It turns **red** when none is. With three or more nodes on a gateway, it is amber when only one node serves it."]],
      ["h", "Which nodes serve a gateway"],
      "Every node of the cluster serves every gateway unless it has been removed from it. A node that has just joined the cluster starts out removed from all of them, so add it where it should serve (right-click the gateway ▸ **Add node**). A gateway you create afterwards is served by every node.",
      ["ul", ["**Remove** (right-click a node) makes it give up the gateway's address and stop answering NTP for it. It is no longer drawn on this gateway on any node. The others carry on.", "It is a shared setting, kept with the gateway, so you can change it from any node and it survives restarts.", "It is not a pause. A pause is for maintenance and is kept on the node itself (or on all nodes). Removing a node is the gateway's permanent list of who serves it.", "Other gateways on the node are not affected. The last node serving a gateway cannot be removed (pause the gateway on all nodes instead)."]],
      ["h", "In a cluster"],
      "The drawing is shared by the whole cluster and can be edited from any node. Pausing a gateway is per node. To work on another member, pick it in the **Node** menu in the top right.",
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Every field of the forms on this page. Right-click the circle ▸ *Edit* (or **＋ New gateway**) for the first form.",
      ["h", "Gateway form"],
      ["fields", [["Group number", "1–255. Names the gateway: nodes that use the same number are one gateway, which is how a node joins an existing one. Chosen for you (the lowest unused number) and fixed once the gateway exists."], ["Name (optional)", "A label shown in the sidebar and on the circle instead of the address. Shared by every node in the cluster. Empty means the address is shown."], ["Shared IPv4 address / prefix", "The address clients send NTP requests to (the VIP), with its prefix length, for example `10.0.0.1/24`. A node serves the gateway only if its interface has an address in that subnet. Shared by the cluster."], ["Shared IPv6 address / prefix (optional)", "The same for IPv6, for example `2001:db8::1/64`. Leave empty for an IPv4-only gateway."], ["More IPv4 addresses (same subnet, optional)", "Further shared addresses inside the VIP's subnet, separated by commas, without a prefix length (`10.0.0.2, 10.0.0.3`). They move with the gateway together with the VIP and answer NTP the same way. The VIP stays the one the nodes use to find each other. Up to 32. Shared by the cluster."], ["More IPv6 addresses (same subnet, optional)", "The same for IPv6 (`2001:db8::2`). Shared by the cluster."], ["Network interface", "The network card this node puts the VIP on (default `eth0`). It belongs to this node only: other cluster nodes keep their own."], ["Fallback NTP servers (optional)", "Servers, separated by commas, in the same forms as the upstream servers (`10.0.0.124`, `ntp.example.com`). Asked only while every server of this gateway is down or paused, and dropped as soon as one answers again. They are never probed."], ["Health checks", "**follow Settings** uses the health checks of Configure ▸ NTP ▸ Health. **own values** uses the four values below for this gateway's servers only. The fields below take effect only with *own values*."], ["Highest stratum", "1–15, default 15. A server that answers with a higher stratum counts as down."], ["Largest root distance (ms)", "0–60000, default 1500; 0 turns the check off. A server whose root distance is larger counts as down."], ["Falseticker offset (ms, 0 = off)", "0–3600000, default 128. With three or more servers, one whose clock differs from the median by more than this counts as down. 0 turns the check off."], ["Failures before down", "Whole number from 1, default 2. Failed probe rounds in a row before a server is marked down."]]],
      ["h", "NTP server form (Add NTP server / Edit server)"],
      ["fields", [["IP address or host name", "The server chrony takes its time from. Add `:port` for a port other than 123. Typing an address looks up its name, and typing a host name here fills the address."], ["Name (optional)", "A label of up to 40 characters, shown under the address on the square. Filled in from the reverse DNS record when you enter an address; yours is kept once you change it."]]],
      ["h", "AnyName address form (Add AnyName address)"],
      ["fields", [["AnyName anycast address", "Pick one of the anycast addresses the AnyName DNS Director on this host announces and that no gateway answers on yet. The gateway then also answers NTP on it, with its own servers. AnyName holds and announces the address; AnyTime only listens. Shared."]]],
      ["h", "Anycast form (Add anycast address / Edit anycast address)"],
      ["fields", [["Anycast address", "A plain IPv4 or IPv6 address of any subnet, without a prefix. It cannot be loopback, link-local, multicast or an address another gateway already uses. Every node running the gateway holds it on `lo` while an NTP server answers."]]],
      ["h", "Statistics windows"],
      ["fields", [["Failed probes", "A server's window: the share of its own probes that failed in each period. Only probes count here, because the probes decide whether the server is up. Not shown for a period with no probes."], ["Time range", "The buttons **Last hour**, **Last day** and **Last week** choose how much history the graphs show. They are per window. The history kept is 7 days."]]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --canvas\nntpdir --canvas-pause gateway|server|anycast ... --scope node|all\nntpdir --canvas-add gateway --vip 10.0.0.1/24 --interface eth0\nntpdir --canvas-add server --group 1 --server 10.0.0.123\nntpdir --canvas-set gateway --group 1 --vip 10.0.0.2/24\nntpdir --canvas-set gateway --group 1 --label \"Office NTP\"   (--label - clears it)\nntpdir --canvas-add fallback --group 1 --server 10.0.0.124   (--canvas-del fallback removes it)\nntpdir --canvas-add anycast --group 1 --address 203.0.113.123\nntpdir --canvas-add dnsdirvip --group 1 --address 203.0.113.53   (answer NTP on an anycast address AnyName announces; --canvas-del dnsdirvip stops)\nntpdir --canvas-del anycast --group 1 --address 203.0.113.123\nntpdir --canvas-set gateway --group 1 --anycast 203.0.113.123,2001:db8:123::1   (replaces the whole list; - clears it)\nntpdir --canvas-del server --group 1 --server 10.0.0.123\nntpdir --canvas-set gateway --group 1 --max-stratum 6   (this gateway's own health checks; also --max-stratum, --max-root-distance, --max-offset MS|off, --fail-threshold; --lb settings follows Settings again)\nntpdir --canvas-move server --group 1 --server 10.0.0.123 --to 1   (also anycast … --address ADDR: the drag in the drawing)\nntpdir --host-lookup 10.0.0.123   (its reverse name; or give a name for its address: what the Add NTP server form fills in)\nntpdir --server-stats 10.0.0.123   (how that server has answered: round trip, offset and loss; --stats-range 1h|1d|7d)\nntpdir --gateway-stats 1   (the gateway of group 1: answer time, errors, availability, requests)\nntpdir --canvas-del node --group 1 --node ntp02   (remove a node from a gateway; --node is its address or host name; --canvas-add node puts it back)\nntpdir --test-vmac [--group 1]   (do virtual MACs reach this node? changes nothing)\nntpdir --canvas-set gateway --group 1 --real-macs on   (run without virtual MACs on every node; off goes back)\nntpdir --canvas-pause gateway --group 1\nntpdir --canvas-resume server --group 1 --server 10.0.0.123\nntpdir --canvas-set server --group 1 --server 10.0.0.123 --label ntp-a   (a name shown instead of the address; - clears)"],
    ],
  },
  gateways: {
    title: "Monitor: Gateways",
    body: [
      "A table of the gateway protocol: for each gateway group and address family, which nodes take part and what each one is doing. It refreshes every 2 seconds. A star (★) marks this node.",
      "Every gateway is listed, whichever node the Node menu has picked. One that the picked node does not run is shown the way a node that serves it sees it (nothing is starred).",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Check which node is the **gateway controller** (it owns the shared address).", "See whether every node that should take part is heard, and whether its hellos are arriving on time.", "See whether each node is actually answering NTP on the address."]],
      ["h", "Reading the table"],
      ["ul", ["**Node name** is the node's host name, taken from the cluster. **Node IP** is the address the node uses in the gateway protocol. The name shows – for a node the cluster does not know, or until that node runs a version that reports its addresses.", "**AGC** (active gateway controller) is the one node that owns the shared address and answers ARP and neighbour requests for it. **AFN** nodes forward traffic and share the load. A node that is only listening, or that has not been heard from yet, has no role.", "**Pri** is the election priority (higher wins). **Slot** is the node's virtual-MAC slot: slot 1 always belongs to the controller, and other nodes take 2 and up. **Weight** is its share of client traffic.", "**State** is where the node is in the protocol. *active* is the controller. *forward* is a forwarder. *standby* is a node that has no slot yet (a node still in an election shows as standby too). *expired* is a node not heard from within the hold time. The node you are on shows its own exact state (listen, speak…). **preempt** marks nodes that take the role back as soon as they outrank the current controller.", "**Hello avg / max** is what the hellos really do, measured here: the average gap between a node's hellos, and the largest gap in the last 30 to 60 seconds. The largest gap includes a silence that is still going on, so a node that has gone quiet shows at once. The values are in milliseconds; compare them with the gateway's hello interval and hold time. A maximum close to the hold time means hellos are being lost or delayed and the node is close to being declared gone. It is empty until two hellos have arrived, and for this node itself.", "**NTP** says whether the node answers NTP on the address (“answering”) or only has it enabled."]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["If nothing is listed on a network that should have peers, check the group ID, the HMAC key and the interface on both nodes. Also check that multicast (or the unicast neighbour list in Configure ▸ Gateways) can pass between them."]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --show-gateways\nntpdir --show-neighbors"],
    ],
  },
  nodes: {
    title: "Monitor: Cluster",
    body: [
      "A read-only list of every node that shares settings with this one. Adding, removing and promoting nodes is done on **Operate ▸ Cluster**; this page only watches.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      "Check that every node is reachable and running the same version, and see who the primary is. The list shows:",
      ["ul", ["Each node's **name** and its **IP** addresses (IPv4, IPv6 global and IPv6 unique local). Hover the name for the node's cluster address.", "Its **role**: primary or replica.", "Whether it is **reachable**, and its **epoch**.", "The version it is **running** and the version of the **source** it holds. They differ while an update is under way.", "How far its clock is from this node's (**Clock vs this node**).", "When it was **last seen**.", "The node you are logged in to is marked with a star (★)."]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --cluster-status"],
    ],
  },
  ntp: {
    title: "Monitor: NTP",
    body: [
      "The live state of chrony on this node and of each gateway's group of upstream time servers. It refreshes every 2 seconds.",
      "Every gateway is listed, whichever node the Node menu has picked. One that the picked node does not run is shown the way a node that serves it sees it. Chrony and its request counts at the top are the picked node's own.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Check that chrony is running and synchronised, and what stratum clients will see.", "See which upstream servers pass the health checks and which one chrony follows.", "See how far each server's clock is from this node's, how fast it answers and why a test failed.", "Check authentication tags for servers that use NTS or a key, and the client rate limit."]],
      ["h", "chrony"],
      "The top row is what chrony says about itself (`chronyc tracking`):",
      ["ul", ["Whether it runs and is **synchronised**.", "Its **Stratum**, which is the one clients see.", "How far the node's **System clock** is from the time chrony believes.", "The server it is **Synchronised to**.", "The **Requests** it has handled, and how many were **Dropped** (rate limited) since it started."]],
      ["note", "A node whose chrony is not running or not synchronised does not announce its anycast addresses, and shows “not answering” on its gateways."],
      ["h", "How servers are chosen"],
      ["ul", ["Every server is probed on a schedule with an NTP request of its own. A probe is judged good when the server answers with the right origin timestamp, is synchronised (not stratum 16 or leap indicator 3), is at or below the **highest stratum**, has a **root distance** within the limit, and has a **round trip** within the slowest allowed. A kiss-o'-death answer (DENY, RSTR, RATE) counts as a failure.", "A server goes **down** after the configured number of failed rounds in a row, and comes back with the first round that passes.", "A server named by host name is probed over **IPv4 and IPv6** (each family it has an address for). It is up when either family passes, and **IPv4 is preferred**: the round trip, offset and stratum shown are IPv4's whenever IPv4 passes, and IPv6's only when it does not. The small **v4** and **v6** marks beside a server's address show both families (green: the one the numbers come from; red: it failed, hover for the reason). A server given as an address is probed over that address's family only.", "With three or more servers, the offsets found are compared. A server whose clock differs from the median by more than **Falseticker offset** is a **falseticker** and is left out until it agrees again (0 turns the check off).", "Every few seconds the servers that pass, across all the groups on the node, are written to a file chrony reads (`/etc/chrony/ntpdir.sources.d/ntpdir.sources`), and chrony is told to read it again. A server that stops passing is gone from that file at the next look, so chrony stops using it. One that passes again is added. chrony itself still picks among the servers it has by its own rules.", "If no server passes, chrony keeps the sources it has, and the fallback servers are used only then. With no fallback, the gateway is red and the node answers no clients (chrony is told to allow nobody) until a server passes again."]],
      ["h", "Reading the table"],
      ["fields", [["Server name", "The name given to the server on the Topology page (right-click the server ▸ Edit). A dash means it has none."], ["Server IP", "The server as configured. A small tag after it shows authentication for a server set to use NTS or a key (Configure ▸ NTP ▸ Security): green **NTS** or **key** when chrony is authenticating with it (hover for details, such as the number of NTS cookies); red with a cross when it is not working (NTS key establishment failed, or the key is refused); grey with dots while chrony has not been restarted with the setting yet (the server is not used until it has). Servers that are not set to be authenticated have no tag."], ["Stratum", "From the last probe. Hover it for the reference ID."], ["Offset", "The server's clock minus this node's, from the last probe."], ["Root dist.", "The server's root distance, from the last probe."], ["Round trip", "The smoothed round trip."], ["Last", "The latest round trip."], ["OK/Fail", "How many probes passed and failed."], ["In chrony", "chrony's own mark for the server: *synchronised to* (the one it follows), *combined*, *not combined*, *not reachable*, *falseticker* or *too variable*. *no* means it is not one of chrony's sources (it is down, paused, or a fallback not in use)."], ["Last error", "Why the last probe failed."]]],
      "**Fallback servers** (marked *fallback* in the list; set in Configure ▸ NTP ▸ Servers, or *Edit* on the Topology page) are used by chrony only while every normal server is down. The header says so.",
      ["h", "Client rate limiting"],
      "The settings are in Configure ▸ NTP ▸ Clients. They go into a small file for chrony (`allow`, `deny`, `ratelimit`).",
      ["ul", ["**Allowed clients** are the networks that may ask. Empty means everyone.", "**Blocked clients** are never answered, whatever the allowed list says (chrony `deny`). Statistics ▸ Top Clients ▸ right-click ▸ **Block** adds one, and a 🚫 marks it.", "**Requests per second** limits each client to a rate, with a **burst**. *kod* lets chrony answer a limited client with a RATE kiss-o'-death more often than *drop* does.", "A changed rate limit restarts chrony, node by node. A new allowed list is applied to the running chrony at once. This node itself is always allowed. Rates are per node. chrony rounds the rate to a power of two and limits per client address mask, as `ratelimit` in its manual says."]],
      "Servers are edited on the Topology page.",
      ["cli", "ntpdir --show-ntp"],
    ],
  },
  config_groups: {
    title: "Configure: Gateways",
    body: [
      "One card for every gateway. A gateway is a shared address (the *VIP*) that clients send NTP requests to; the nodes of the cluster agree between themselves who answers for it. This page holds the technical settings of that agreement.",
      ["note", "Gateways are **added and deleted on the Topology page**, not here. Use this page to tune one that already exists. If you are unsure about a value, leave it as it is: the defaults suit most networks."],
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Set which network card a gateway uses on this node.", "Set this node's priority and weight, and whether it takes the controller role back when it returns.", "Choose how clients are shared over the nodes.", "Tune the hello and hold timers.", "Change the key that signs the gateway messages.", "List the nodes by address when multicast cannot pass between them."]],
      ["h", "How the nodes agree on who answers"],
      ["ul", ["Every node of a gateway sends short *hello* messages. One node becomes the **gateway controller (AGC)** and owns the shared address. The other nodes, the **forwarders (AFN)**, share the traffic.", "The node with the highest **priority** is chosen as controller. If two are equal, the higher address wins.", "If a node goes quiet for longer than the **hold time**, the others treat it as gone and carry on without it."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: make one node the preferred controller"],
      ["ol", ["On that node, open **Configure ▸ Gateways** and find the gateway's card.", "Raise **Priority** above the other nodes' priorities (for example 200 against 100).", "Tick **Preemption**, so the node takes the role back whenever it is up. Leave it unticked if you would rather it waited until the current controller fails."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: change the key"],
      ["ol", ["Open the gateway's card and edit **HMAC shared key**.", "Save by leaving the field. The cluster pushes the new key to every node, and each node restarts the gateway.", "Expect the nodes to lose sight of each other for a moment. Do this at a quiet time."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: use a list of nodes instead of multicast"],
      ["ol", ["In a network that blocks multicast (a cloud network, for example), open **Neighbors (unicast mode)**.", "Add the address of **every** node of the gateway, this one included, one to a row.", "The nodes now talk to each other directly. The list is shared, so you only enter it once."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["Name", "Optional label shown instead of the address. Shared."], ["Group ID", "1–255. Nodes with the same ID are one gateway; the ID also keys the election. Shared."], ["Interface", "The network card this node uses for the gateway (default `eth0`). This node only."], ["IPv4 VIP/prefix", "The shared IPv4 address and prefix, for example `10.0.0.1/24`. Empty turns IPv4 off for the gateway. Shared."], ["IPv6 VIP/prefix", "The shared IPv6 address and prefix. Empty turns IPv6 off. Shared."], ["More IPv4 addresses", "Further shared addresses in the VIP's subnet, one to a row of a table, without a prefix length. They fail over with the VIP. Shared."], ["More IPv6 addresses", "The same for IPv6. Shared."], ["Priority", "0–255, default 100. The election: the node with the highest priority becomes the active gateway controller (ties go to the higher address). This node only."], ["LB method", "How clients are spread over the forwarders: `roundrobin` (default; each new request goes to the next forwarder), `weighted` (in proportion to each node's weight), `hostpinned` (a client always gets the same forwarder) or `failover` (one forwarder takes everything, the rest stand by). Shared."], ["Weight", "0–255, default 100. This node's share of traffic under the `weighted` method; ignored by the others. This node only."], ["Hello interval (ms)", "How often a node announces itself, default 150. It cannot be larger than the hold time. Shared."], ["Hold time (ms)", "How long a neighbour may stay silent before it is considered gone, default 750. It cannot be smaller than the hello interval; keep it at least three times the hello interval, or one lost hello can look like a failure. Shared."], ["Max forwarders", "1–255, default 128. The most nodes that take part as forwarders (AFN). Shared."], ["HMAC shared key", "The secret that signs the gateway protocol messages. A new gateway starts with a random key. Every node of a gateway must use the same key, and a node with another key is ignored. Change it here and the cluster pushes it to every node, which restarts the gateway; for a moment the nodes do not hear each other, so do it at a quiet time. A key that is well known (`dnsdir`, `change-me`) or shorter than 12 characters is marked in red: anyone on the network who knows it can take the gateway over. Messages also carry the sender's clock and are dropped when it is more than 30 seconds from the receiver's or when they are not newer than the last one from that node, so a recorded message cannot be played back; keep the nodes' clocks in step (NTP). Shared."], ["Preemption", "Ticked: a node with a higher priority takes the active role back from the current controller as soon as it is up. Unticked: it waits until the controller fails. This node only."], ["Use real MAC addresses (no virtual MACs)", "On is the default for a gateway you add (gateways that already existed keep their setting). Off is the older way: each forwarder has a virtual MAC and the controller covers a dead node's MAC. On: no virtual MACs at all. Every node holds the VIP on its loopback, the controller answers ARP and IPv6 neighbor requests with the **real** MAC of the node it picks (it learns the others' MACs from their frames and asks for the ones it has not heard), and nothing is taken over when a node dies: the controller announces the VIP at its own MAC with an unsolicited ARP/neighbor advertisement, which only the neighbors that honor one will follow; the rest keep sending to the dead node until their ARP entry ages out. Needed where virtual MACs cannot work: a VMware port group with promiscuous mode off, a cloud that allows one MAC per interface. Shared by the cluster; changing it restarts the gateway on each node. The vMAC column of the Gateways page then shows real MAC addresses (the controller knows them all, a forwarder only its own)."], ["Neighbors (unicast mode)", "One IP address to a row of a table: list **every** node of the gateway, this one too (each node skips its own address). With none (the default) the nodes find each other by multicast; use the list when multicast cannot pass between them, such as in a cloud network. Shared: it replicates to every node."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["Keep the hold time at least three times the hello interval. Otherwise one lost hello can look like a failure.", "A key that is well known or short (under 12 characters) is marked in red. Anyone on the network who knows it can take the gateway over.", "The Gateways page (Monitor ▸ Gateways) shows what the nodes are doing with these settings."]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --show-config\nntpdir --show-gateways\nntpdir --show-neighbors"],
    ],
  },
  config_logs: {
    title: "Configure: Settings ▸ Logs",
    body: [
      "The **Logs** tab of **Configure ▸ Settings** says how much detail the log records and, if you want, sends a copy of the log to a syslog server so it can be kept and searched with the rest of your logs.",
      "**Every change saves and applies as soon as you leave the field or tick the box.** There is no Save button. If AnyTime refuses a value, a red *Not saved* message says why and nothing is changed until you correct it.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Choose how much detail the log records.", "Send the log to a syslog server over TCP, UDP or TLS.", "Check that the server is reachable, and send it a test message."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: change the log level"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Configure ▸ Settings ▸ Logs**.", "Pick a value in **Log level**. Use `info` day to day and `debug` only while you are chasing a problem.", "That is all: the new level is used straight away. Read the log under **Monitor ▸ Log**."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: send the log to a syslog server"],
      ["ol", ["In the **Syslog** card, type the **Server**: a host name or an address, with the port if it is not the usual one.", "Pick the **Protocol**. **tls** is encrypted and the one to choose across a network you do not control; **tcp** does not lose messages while the connection is up; **udp** is the simplest and can lose messages silently.", "For **tls**, either give the **CA certificate file** that signed the server's certificate, or leave it empty to trust the system's authorities. Ticking **Do not check the server's certificate** turns the check off: the traffic is still encrypted, but anyone can pretend to be the server.", "Pick the **Facility** your collector expects (`daemon` is the default; `local0` to `local7` are for sorting a product's logs apart) and the **Message format**.", "Tick **Send the log to a syslog server**. The line under the settings shows *connected* once the server has accepted the connection.", "Press **Send test message** and look for it on the server."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["Log level", "`debug`, `info` (default), `warning` or `error`. The least important lines the daemon writes to the journal and the Log page."], ["Send the log to a syslog server", "Switches forwarding on or off. Your settings stay when it is off. Forwarding is per node: tick it on every node that should send its log."], ["Protocol", "How messages travel. `tcp` (the default) keeps a connection and reconnects by itself; `udp` sends one datagram per message with no check that it arrived; `tls` is `tcp` inside an encrypted, authenticated connection (the usual port is 6514)."], ["Server", "The syslog server: a host name or an address, with `:port` after it if the port is not the usual one (514, or 6514 for `tls`). An IPv6 address with a port is written in square brackets: `[2001:db8::9]:514`."], ["Facility", "The syslog facility the messages are filed under. `daemon` is right for most; `local0` to `local7` let the collector sort this product's messages from everything else."], ["Message format", "`rfc5424` (the default) is the modern format with a precise time stamp. Choose `rfc3164` only for an old collector that cannot read it."], ["CA certificate file (tls)", "The PEM file, on this node, of the authority that signed the server's certificate. Empty trusts the system's authorities. Used only with `tls`."], ["Do not check the server's certificate (tls)", "Accept any certificate the server shows. The connection is still encrypted, but it is no longer proof that you reached your server. Use it to try things out, not for good. Used only with `tls`."]]],
      ["h", "What is sent"],
      ["ul", ["Every line the log shows, one syslog message per line, at the severity of the line: debug, info, warning and error become the matching syslog severities. The **Log level** above decides what is in the log, and so what is sent.", "The program name is `ntpdir` and the host name is this node's.", "On `tcp` and `tls`, an `rfc5424` message is sent with its length in front (RFC 6587 octet counting) and an `rfc3164` message ends with a new line. On `udp` each message is one datagram."]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["A server that is down never holds AnyTime up. Messages wait in a queue; if it fills (2000 messages) the oldest are dropped and counted next to *sent* in the status line. The connection is retried every few seconds, slowing to every 30.", "The log is also still written to the journal and to `ntpdir.log` in the state directory, whatever happens to the syslog server. That file is rotated at 100 MB with the two previous files kept.", "The settings are for this node only and are not copied to the other nodes."]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --syslog\nntpdir --syslog-server logs.example.com --syslog-protocol tls --syslog-ca /etc/ssl/log-ca.pem\nntpdir --syslog-facility local3 --syslog-format rfc5424\nntpdir --syslog-disable | --syslog-enable\nntpdir --syslog-test"],
    ],
  },
  config_upgrades: {
    title: "Configure: Settings ▸ Upgrades",
    body: [
      "The **Upgrades** tab of **Configure ▸ Settings** decides how AnyTime keeps itself up to date: whether a release you stage is installed everywhere by itself, and where **Fetch from online** downloads releases from. Staging and applying a release is done on **Operate ▸ Upgrade**.",
      "**Every change saves and applies as soon as you leave the field or tick the box.** There is no Save button.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Switch automatic updating on or off.", "Point updates at a different online repository (for example a fork)."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: turn automatic updates off"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Configure ▸ Settings ▸ Upgrades**.", "Untick **Update every node automatically…**.", "Confirm the question. From now on a staged release waits until you press the update button on **Operate ▸ Upgrade**."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: use a different online repository"],
      ["ol", ["Type the address of the repository in **Online repository** (for example your own fork), and leave the field.", "Leave the field empty to go back to the default."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["Update every node automatically when a newer release is staged (one node at a time)", "Auto-update. On (the default), each node builds and installs a staged release by itself, one node at a time (the first node must run it 2 minutes without trouble before the others follow), and rolls back if the new version does not stay up. Ticking it asks you to confirm. Applies to the whole cluster."], ["Online repository", "The GitHub repository **Fetch from online** (Operate ▸ Upgrade) downloads releases from, written as `https://github.com/owner/repo` (the default is `https://github.com/micush/ntpdir`; use it to follow a fork or if the repository moves). Saved when you leave the field; an empty field goes back to the default. Only github.com repositories are accepted. Shared: it is kept with the update settings on the primary and followed by every node. Nodes build and install what is downloaded as root, so point it only at a repository you trust. CLI: `--update-repo URL|default`."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["Automatic updating is **on** by default, and it applies to the whole cluster. Nodes update one at a time, and the first one must run the new version for two minutes without trouble before the rest follow.", "Nodes build and install a downloaded release as root. Point the online repository only at a repository you trust."]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --update-auto on|off\nntpdir --update-repo URL|default"],
    ],
  },
  config_ntp0: {
    title: "Configure: NTP ▸ Servers",
    body: [
      "The upstream time servers. On each node, chrony takes its time from the servers that pass the health checks (see **Monitor ▸ NTP**). A gateway you draw on the Topology page can add servers of its own; every node uses the servers of all its gateways together with these.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["List the time servers your nodes use.", "List fallback servers, used only when every ordinary server is down."]],
      ["h", "How to write a server"],
      ["ul", ["A host name: `ntp.example.com`.", "An address: `10.0.0.123`, or `[2001:db8::123]:123` for IPv6.", "Add `:port` for a port other than 123, for example `10.0.1.123:1123`."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: add a server"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Configure ▸ NTP ▸ Servers**.", "Add a row to **Servers** and type the host name or address.", "Leave the field. It is saved and tested at once. Watch it appear on **Monitor ▸ NTP**."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["Servers", "The upstream servers, one to a row of a table: `10.0.0.123`, `10.0.1.123:1123`, `[2001:db8::123]:123` or `ntp.example.com`. Shared. Used by the gateways that have no servers of their own; a node's chrony uses only the servers of the topology."], ["Fallback servers", "Same forms. Used by chrony only while no server above passes, never probed, and always counted as up. Shared."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["Use three or more servers where you can: with fewer, a wrong clock cannot be spotted by comparing.", "Fallback servers are never tested. They are treated as up and used by chrony only while no ordinary server passes.", "chrony is set up by the installer and answers clients on the gateway addresses.", "A node's chrony uses only the servers of the topology, so a server added here is used by gateways that have none of their own."]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --show-config\nntpdir --configure       (interactive wizard)"],
    ],
  },
  config_ntp1: {
    title: "Configure: NTP ▸ Health",
    body: [
      "When a time server counts as down. Every server is tested on a schedule. One that fails is left out of chrony's sources until it passes again. **Monitor ▸ NTP** shows the result live.",
      "These values apply to every gateway unless a gateway has its own (Topology ▸ right-click the gateway ▸ Edit ▸ Health checks ▸ own values).",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Decide how far a server may be from perfect before it is ignored.", "Decide how far this node's own clock may drift before it stops serving.", "Set how often servers are tested."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: tighten the checks"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Configure ▸ NTP ▸ Health**.", "Lower **Highest stratum** to refuse servers far from a reference clock (stratum 1 is a server attached to a reference clock; each step away adds one).", "Lower **Largest root distance (ms)** or **Slowest round trip (ms, 0 = off)** to refuse servers that are far away or slow.", "Watch **Monitor ▸ NTP**: servers that fail show red with the reason in *Last error*."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["Highest stratum", "1–15, default 15. A server that answers with a higher stratum counts as down."], ["Largest root distance (ms)", "0–60000, default 1500; 0 turns the check off. A server whose root distance is larger counts as down."], ["Slowest round trip (ms, 0 = off)", "0–60000, default 500; 0 turns the check off. A server whose probe takes longer than this is too slow and counts as down. Shared."], ["Serve only within (ms, 0 = off)", "0–3600000, default 50; 0 turns the check off. A node serves, and keeps the gateway address and any anycast address, only while its clock is within this many milliseconds of what chrony measures against the servers (the offset on the circle's tooltip). Beyond it the node answers nobody and leaves, like a node whose chrony is down, and comes back once it is within 80% of the limit; the other nodes carry on. In a container this is the host's clock against the servers. Shared."], ["Falseticker offset (ms, 0 = off)", "0–3600000, default 128. With three or more servers, one whose clock differs from the median by more than this counts as down. 0 turns the check off."], ["Failures before down", "Whole number from 1, default 2. Failed probe rounds in a row before a server is marked down."], ["Probe interval (ms)", "From 1, default 16000. How often each server is probed. Shared."], ["Probe timeout (ms)", "From 1, default 1000. How long a probe waits before it counts as failed. Shared."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["A server goes down only after the number of failed rounds in **Failures before down** in a row, and comes back with the first round that passes.", "**Serve only within** protects clients from a node whose own clock is wrong: beyond the limit the node leaves, like a node whose chrony is down, and comes back once it is within 80% of the limit. The other nodes carry on.", "A *falseticker* needs at least three servers to be found.", "A kiss-o'-death answer from a server (DENY, RSTR, RATE) counts as a failure."]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --show-config\nntpdir --configure       (interactive wizard)"],
    ],
  },
  config_ntp2: {
    title: "Configure: NTP ▸ Clients",
    body: [
      "How clients reach chrony and how much they may ask. These settings go into a small file that chrony reads (`allow`, `deny`, `ratelimit`, `port`).",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Change the NTP port.", "Allow only certain networks.", "Block a client or network.", "Limit how fast any one client may ask."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: allow only your own networks"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Configure ▸ NTP ▸ Clients**.", "Add your networks to **Allowed clients** (`10.0.0.0/8`, `192.168.1.5`, `2001:db8::/32`).", "An empty list allows everyone. This node itself is always allowed. The change reaches the running chrony at once."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: block one client"],
      ["ol", ["Add its address to **Blocked clients**, or use **Monitor ▸ Statistics ▸ Top Clients ▸ right-click ▸ Block**.", "A block wins over the allowed list, even when it names a network inside an allowed one. It applies at once without a restart.", "To undo it, remove the row, or choose **Unblock** in Top Clients."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: limit the request rate"],
      ["ol", ["Set **Requests per second** (0 means no limit) and, if you like, **Burst (requests at once)**.", "Choose **Over the limit**: `drop` (mostly send nothing) or `kod` (answer a limited client with a RATE kiss-o'-death more often, which asks it to slow down).", "Saving restarts chrony, one node at a time (see below)."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["NTP port (UDP, chrony)", "1–65535, default 123. The port chrony answers NTP on (chrony's `port`). Shared."], ["Allowed clients", "Networks that may ask, one to a row of a table (`10.0.0.0/8`, `192.168.1.5`, `2001:db8::/32`). Empty (the default) allows everyone; this node itself is always allowed. Shared."], ["Blocked clients", "Clients and networks that get no answers, one to a row of a table (`192.168.5.14`, `10.9.0.0/16`, `2001:db8::5`). A block wins over Allowed clients, also when it names a network inside an allowed one. Written to chrony as `deny` and applied at once, without a restart. This node itself cannot be blocked. Statistics ▸ Top Clients ▸ right-click ▸ **Block** adds an address here and **Unblock** removes it. Shared."], ["Requests per second", "0 (the default) means no limit. chrony rounds the rate to a power of two. Shared."], ["Burst (requests at once)", "How many requests a client may send at once. 0 means twice the rate, at least 10. Shared."], ["Over the limit", "`drop` (default; mostly send nothing) or `kod` (chrony answers a limited client with a RATE kiss-o'-death more often, which asks it to slow down). Shared."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["A changed port or rate limit restarts chrony. The nodes do it one at a time: each is paused, chrony is restarted, the node waits until chrony is synchronised, then it is resumed. A node that is alone cannot be paused and has no NTP for the few seconds chrony needs.", "If chrony does not synchronise within 3 minutes the node is resumed, shows *restart failed* on this page, and the others wait.", "chrony rounds the rate to a power of two. The rate limit applies per client address, and counts are per node."]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --show-config\nntpdir --configure       (interactive wizard)"],
    ],
  },
  config_ntp3: {
    title: "Configure: NTP ▸ Security",
    body: [
      "Authenticating time, so that a client or a node can be sure an answer really came from the server it asked. There are two ways: **symmetric keys** (a shared secret between two machines) and **NTS** (Network Time Security, RFC 8915: TLS certificates instead of shared secrets, usable across the internet).",
      ["note", "A change here restarts chrony on every node, one at a time (**Operate ▸ Node** shows the progress), the same as a new port does. chrony only reads these files when it starts."],
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Keep a list of symmetric keys, so clients that sign their requests get signed answers.", "Make chrony use NTS or a key when it talks to an upstream server.", "Serve NTS to your clients."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: use NTS with a public server"],
      ["ol", ["Make sure the server is in the Servers list (**Configure ▸ NTP ▸ Servers**), written the same way.", "Open **Configure ▸ NTP ▸ Security** and add a row to **Authenticated servers**: the server, the method `nts`.", "Wait for chrony to restart on each node. The **Server IP** column on Monitor ▸ NTP then shows a green **NTS** tag."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: use a key with a server"],
      ["ol", ["Add the key to **Keys**: an ID (a whole number), a hash, and the key.", "Add a row to **Authenticated servers**: the server, the method `key`, and the key's ID.", "The server must have the same key."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: serve NTS to your clients"],
      ["ol", ["Install a certificate for your gateway's name on **Configure ▸ Web GUI ▸ Certificates**.", "Tick **Serve NTS to clients**.", "Open TCP 4460 in the firewall. Clients must connect by a name the certificate has."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["Keys", "Symmetric keys, a table with the columns **Key ID**, **Hash** and **Key**, which chrony's key file has as `ID HASH KEY` (`7 SHA256 HEX:5e0c…`, `8 MD5 mysecret`). The ID is a whole number from 1; the hash is MD5, SHA1, SHA256, SHA384, SHA512, SHA3-224…SHA3-512 or RMD160; the key is up to 64 printable characters (no `#`), or `HEX:` and an even number of hex digits. At most 64 keys. A client that signs its request with one of these keys gets a signed answer; nothing else has to be set here to serve it. The keys are kept in the config file and its history, readable by root only, and left out of support bundles; the history says only how many there are. Shared."], ["Authenticated servers", "How chrony reaches an upstream server, a table with the columns **Server**, **Method** (`nts` or `key`) and **Key ID** (for `key`), stored as `time.cloudflare.com nts` (NTS) or `10.0.0.5 key 7` (key 7 from the list above). The server must also be in the Servers list (or a gateway's own list), written the same way (a port, if any, as there). ntpdir's own health probe is a plain NTP request, so the server must answer plain NTP as well as NTS or the key (most public NTS servers do). While chrony has not yet been restarted with a new key or NTS setting, an authenticated server stays out of chrony's sources rather than being used without authentication. NTS needs a chrony 4.0 or newer built with NTS and a clock close enough to check certificates (about a day). A server cannot use both. Shared."], ["Serve NTS to clients", "Also serve NTS: chrony answers NTS key establishment on TCP 4460 (open that port in the firewall) with this node's web GUI certificate (Configure ▸ Web GUI), which is copied to a file chrony can read and followed when it is renewed. Clients must connect by a name the certificate has, so install a certificate for your gateway's name there; a client that does not trust its issuer refuses it. Each node keeps its own NTS cookie keys, so a client that moves to another node (anycast failover) has to set up NTS again, which chrony and most clients do by themselves. Shared."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["While chrony has not yet been restarted with a new key or NTS setting, an authenticated server stays out of chrony's sources rather than being used without authentication.", "NTS needs chrony 4.0 or newer built with NTS, and a clock close enough to check certificates (about a day).", "ntpdir's own health probe is a plain NTP request, so an authenticated server must answer plain NTP as well. Most public NTS servers do.", "Each node keeps its own NTS cookie keys, so a client that moves to another node (anycast failover) has to set up NTS again. chrony and most clients do this by themselves.", "The keys are kept in the configuration file and its history, readable by root only, and left out of support bundles. The history says only how many there are."]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --show-config\nntpdir --configure       (interactive wizard)"],
    ],
  },
  config_ntp4: {
    title: "Configure: NTP ▸ Leap seconds",
    body: [
      "How chrony treats a leap second: a minute of 61 seconds, announced by the time sources a few months ahead. All of these are shared by the cluster, written to chrony and applied by restarting chrony on every node, one at a time.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Tell chrony where to find the leap second table.", "Choose what the node's own clock does at a leap second.", "Serve clients a smeared time instead of a 61st second."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: use the system's leap second table"],
      ["ol", ["Install the `tzdata` package on every node, so that `/usr/share/zoneinfo/right/UTC` exists.", "Open **Configure ▸ NTP ▸ Leap seconds** and type `right/UTC` into **Leap second table**.", "A node that lacks the file leaves the line out and says so in the Log."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: smear leap seconds"],
      ["ol", ["Tick **Smear leap seconds**.", "Clients are then served a time that is spread over about 17 hours around the leap second. They never see a 61st second."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["Leap second table", "The name of a time zone in the system's tz database that carries leap seconds, normally `right/UTC` (chrony's `leapsectz`). chrony then knows about a leap second from the table as well as from its sources. It must exist on every node (`/usr/share/zoneinfo/right/UTC`, from the tzdata package); a node that lacks it leaves the line out and says so in the Log. Empty (the default) uses what the sources announce. Shared."], ["Leap second mode", "What the node's own clock does at a leap second (chrony's `leapsecmode`): `system` (the default: the kernel inserts or deletes the second), `step` (chrony steps the clock just after it), `slew` (chrony slews the clock to the new time, so it is briefly off by up to a second) or `ignore` (the second is not applied, and the clock is corrected later). Shared."], ["Smear leap seconds", "Serve clients a time that is smeared across the leap second (over about 17 hours, chrony's `smoothtime 400 0.001 leaponly`) instead of repeating a second at midnight. Clients see no 61st second. Only the answers to clients are smeared, not the node's own clock. Do not mix smeared and unsmeared servers in one client's list, and do not use a smeared server as an upstream of another node. Shared."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["Only the answers to clients are smeared, not the node's own clock.", "Do not mix smeared and unsmeared servers in one client's list, and do not use a smeared server as an upstream of another node.", "An empty **Leap second table** (the default) uses what the sources announce."]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --show-config\nntpdir --configure       (interactive wizard)"],
    ],
  },
  config_web: {
    title: "Configure: Web GUI",
    body: [
      "This page has two parts: how people **sign in** to the console (the port it listens on, who may sign in, how long a session lasts), and the **certificate** the console is served with. Both are explained below, one after the other.",
      ["h", "Part 1: Sign-in settings"],
      "Every change saves as you leave the field.",
      ["h", "Sign-in: What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Change the port or address the console listens on.", "Choose which operating-system groups may sign in with full access, and which only to look.", "Set the idle timeout and the lockout rules.", "Set the shortest password the Users page accepts."]],
      ["h", "Sign-in: Step by step: change the port"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Configure ▸ Web GUI ▸ General** and edit **Listen address**.", "Type just a port (for example `12380`) to listen on every address, or an address and port (`10.0.0.5:12380`) to listen on one.", "Confirm the question. The console restarts and **everyone is signed out**. Open the new address in your browser."]],
      ["h", "Sign-in: Step by step: give someone read-only access"],
      ["ol", ["Go to **Users ▸ Local** and add the person, choosing **Read-only** for their access. Or add them to the group named in **Read-only groups** yourself.", "They can now sign in and see every page, but every change is refused."]],
      ["h", "Sign-in: Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["Listen address", "The port the GUI listens on, default `12380`, on every address; to listen on one address only, write it with the port, such as `10.0.0.5:12380` (saved as `listen` in the `web` block, where a bare port is kept as `:12380`). Changing it restarts the GUI and logs everyone out, so you are asked to confirm."], ["Login groups", "The operating-system groups whose members may sign in: one name or several separated by commas (default `ntpdir,ntpdir-users`). A member of any of them may sign in. The first one that is a local group is the one the Users page manages; `ntpdir-users` is meant for a group in Active Directory (or another directory, through sssd), which is kept there. A name that does not exist on a node is skipped. In a cluster this setting is **shared**: it is the same on every node, set on any of them. (A cluster whose login group is still just `ntpdir` shares nothing; the first change to anything else is shared.)"], ["Read-only groups", "Operating-system groups (default `ntpdir-ro`, the local group the installer creates; one name or several separated by commas, local or from a directory; empty turns it off) whose members may sign in **read-only**: they can open every page and see everything, but cannot change anything (every save, button and action is refused with a message, and Sign out still works). Someone who is also in a login group has full access. Keys and passwords in the configuration are shown as (hidden), and the saved configuration versions cannot be opened or downloaded. Add people on the Users page (choose Read-only when adding someone, or change an existing user's Access; the first local group of the setting is the one it manages), or with `usermod -aG ntpdir-ro alice`; a directory group is managed in the directory. The setting is shared by the cluster."], ["PAM service", "The `/etc/pam.d` service that checks passwords (default `ntpdir`). AD users through sssd: if the password is accepted but the log says `pam account check`, the daemon adds `ad_gpo_map_interactive = +ntpdir` to the domain section of `sssd.conf` itself at start (and restarts sssd); if it could not, add it yourself."], ["Session idle timeout (min)", "From 1, default 30. A browser is signed out after this many idle minutes."], ["Failed logins before lockout", "1–1000, default 3. Wrong attempts, within the window below, that lock out that address, and that address together with that user name. A user name alone is never locked out, so nobody can lock an administrator out by failing logins for that name. Shared by the cluster (this and the other lockout settings and the minimum length)."], ["…within this many minutes", "From 1, default 1. The window in which the failed logins are counted."], ["Lockout lasts (minutes)", "From 1, default 15. How long the lockout lasts."], ["Minimum password length", "1–128, default 8 (0 or empty is the default). The fewest characters a password set on the Users page, or with `--user-add` and `--user-password`, may have. Existing passwords are not checked. Changing a user's password, deleting the user or letting the account expire signs that user out of every open session. Shared by the cluster."]]],
      ["h", "Sign-in: The sign-in page"],
      "These two fields are on the sign-in page, not on the Configure page:",
      ["fields", [["Username", "On the sign-in page: an operating-system user who is a member of the login group."], ["Password", "On the sign-in page: that user's password, checked through PAM. After too many wrong attempts the sign-in page is locked for a while."]]],
      ["h", "Sign-in: Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["A **lockout** blocks the address, and that address together with that user name. A user name on its own is never locked out, so nobody can lock an administrator out by guessing at the name.", "Changing or deleting a user, or letting their account expire, signs them out of every open session.", "Single sign-on and directory sign-in are set up on the **Users** page (tabs SSO and LDAP)."]],
      ["h", "Part 2: The certificate"],
      "The certificate this console is served with. Until you install one, AnyTime makes its own *self-signed* certificate, which browsers warn about.",
      "The same certificate is used when you serve NTS to clients (Configure ▸ NTP ▸ Security).",
      ["h", "Certificate: What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Install a certificate you already have.", "Create a certificate request (CSR) for your certificate authority.", "Go back to the previous certificate, or make a fresh self-signed one."]],
      ["h", "Certificate: Step by step: install a certificate you already have"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Configure ▸ Web GUI ▸ Certificates**.", "Paste the certificate into **Certificate** (your own first, then any intermediate ones), or choose the file.", "Paste the key into **Private key**, or choose its file.", "Press install. The pair is checked: the order of the chain, that the key fits, that it is still valid, that it can be used for a server, and its names.", "It is used from the next connection. No restart, and nobody is signed out."]],
      ["h", "Certificate: Step by step: get a certificate from your certificate authority"],
      ["ol", ["Fill in **Main name** (for example `gw.example.com`), and if needed **Other names** and **IP addresses**.", "Create the request. Copy the text from **Pending CSR** and send it to your certificate authority.", "When the certificate comes back, paste it into **Certificate**. Leave **Private key** empty: the key is already on this server.", "Install it."]],
      ["h", "Certificate: Fields"],
      ["fields", [["Certificate", "The certificate in PEM form, your own first and then any intermediate ones. Paste it or choose a file under it (**Certificate file**: `.pem`, `.crt`, `.cer` or `.txt`)."], ["Private key", "Its private key in PEM form, pasted or from a **Key file** (`.pem`, `.key` or `.txt`). Leave it empty when the certificate answers a request created here, because the key is already on this server."], ["Main name", "For a request: the common name, the main host name the certificate is for, for example `gw.example.com`."], ["Other names (one per line)", "For a request: further host names the certificate should cover."], ["IP addresses (one per line, optional)", "For a request: IP addresses the certificate should cover, so it can be used when you open the GUI by address."], ["Pending CSR", "The request that was created, read only. Copy it and send it to your certificate authority."]]],
      ["h", "Certificate: Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["**Revert** goes back to the previous certificate. **Regenerate** makes a new self-signed one.", "In a cluster, the certificate installed on the primary is copied to every member, and reverting it on the primary reverts them all.", "A certificate named in the configuration file overrides anything installed here.", "The page warns you when the certificate runs out within 30 days."]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --show-config\nntpdir --configure       (interactive wizard)\nntpdir --config-import FILE\nntpdir --tls-status\nntpdir --tls-install --cert-file F --key-file F\nntpdir --tls-csr --cn NAME [--san NAME_OR_IP,...]\nntpdir --tls-revert\nntpdir --tls-regenerate"],
    ],
  },
  config_snmp: {
    title: "Configure: SNMP",
    body: [
      "Lets a monitoring system read this node's status over SNMP. Access is **read-only**, over IPv4 and IPv6 on UDP port 161.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Turn SNMP on by adding a community string, and off by removing them all.", "Set the location and the contact the node reports."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: turn SNMP on"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Configure ▸ SNMP** and add a row under **Community strings** (for example a long random word).", "Optionally fill in **Location** and **Contact**.", "AnyTime now writes `/etc/snmp/snmpd.conf` and starts `snmpd` itself. A green **ACTIVE** tag appears beside the heading once it is running on the node picked in the top bar.", "Open UDP 161 in the firewall for your monitoring system, and give it the community string."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: turn SNMP off"],
      ["ol", ["Remove every community string. `snmpd` is switched off. A configuration file that was there before is put back."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["Community strings", "One to a row of a table, up to 16; each gives read-only access over IPv4 and IPv6. SNMP is on while there is at least one and off when the list is empty. Shared. Each string is up to 64 letters, digits or punctuation (no spaces, quotes, backslash or `#`). They are kept in the config file and its history, readable by root only, and left out of support bundles."], ["Location", "What snmpd reports as `sysLocation`, e.g. `Rack 4, Phoenix DC` (one line, up to 255 characters). Optional. Not shared: each node has its own, since it differs from node to node."], ["Contact", "What snmpd reports as `sysContact`, e.g. `noc@example.com` (one line, up to 255 characters). Optional. Shared."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["An `snmpd.conf` that was there before is kept as `snmpd.conf.ntpdir-orig` and put back when the list is emptied. An snmpd that AnyTime never took over is left alone.", "The community strings are kept in the configuration file and its history, readable by root only, and left out of support bundles."]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --show-config"],
    ],
  },
  config_cluster: {
    title: "Configure: Settings ▸ Cluster",
    body: [
      "How this node talks to the other nodes of its cluster: the port it listens on, the address the others use to find it, and how often it checks in. **Joining, promoting and removing nodes is done on Operate ▸ Cluster**, not here.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Change the cluster port or bind it to one address.", "Tell the other nodes which address to use for this one (useful behind NAT or with several addresses).", "Change how often replicas copy the settings."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: set the address the others should use"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Configure ▸ Settings ▸ Cluster** on the node.", "Fill in **Advertised address** as `host:port`, for example `10.0.0.5:12381`.", "Leave it empty to use this host's name with the listen port."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "These belong to this node, not to the cluster.",
      ["fields", [["Binding address", "The port the cluster listens on, which the other nodes connect to. Just the port (default `12381`) listens on all addresses; give an address with it, such as `10.0.0.5:12381`, to bind to one."], ["Advertised address", "`host:port` the other members use to reach this node. Empty means this host's name with the listen port."], ["Sync interval (s)", "1–3600, default 5. How often replicas pull state and members are polled."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["The cluster port (12381 by default) is separate from the console port (12380) and must be reachable between the nodes.", "Nodes pin each other's certificates and sign every request with the cluster secret.", "A shorter sync interval makes changes spread faster but asks the other nodes more often."]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --cluster-status"],
    ],
  },
  anycast_bgp: {
    title: "Configure: Anycast ▸ BGP",
    body: [
      "Announce the gateways' **anycast addresses** to your routers with BGP. This page has two tabs: **BGP** (this one) and **OSPF** (next to it); use either one, or both. AnyTime drives FRR on this node: it writes `/etc/frr/frr.conf`, turns `bgpd` and `bfdd` on while BGP is set up, and reloads FRR. FRR must be installed on the node.",
      ["note", "If the AnyName DNS Director is installed on the same host, BGP and OSPF are **unavailable** here. AnyName manages FRR the same way and the two would overwrite each other's file, so AnyTime leaves FRR alone, ignores any BGP and OSPF settings in its config and refuses to set up or enable either. Anycast addresses still work as plain addresses on `lo`. Removing AnyName lifts this."],
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Set this node's AS number and router ID.", "Add the routers (BGP neighbors) this node talks to.", "Tune the timers and make a node the backup with *AS Prepend*."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: announce your anycast addresses"],
      ["ol", ["Add the anycast addresses to the gateway on the Topology page (right-click the circle ▸ **Add anycast address**).", "Open **Configure ▸ Anycast** on each node. Type this node's **Local AS number**. BGP now runs here.", "Press **+** above the neighbor table and fill in the router's **address** and **AS**. The neighbor is created once it has both.", "Set the matching password, if your router uses one.", "Watch **Monitor ▸ Anycast**: *Established* is a working session."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: make a node the backup"],
      ["ol", ["On that node, tick **AS Prepend**.", "It adds its AS three more times to every announcement. A longer path makes your routers prefer the nodes that do not have it ticked."]],
      ["h", "What it does"],
      ["ul", ["**Per node.** The AS, router ID and neighbors are not shared with the cluster — each node usually peers with its own upstream router. The anycast addresses themselves are shared (right-click a gateway on the Topology page → *Add anycast address*).", "**On when an AS is set.** BGP runs on this node while a local AS number is set. Clear the AS number to turn it off: the BGP section is removed from `frr.conf` and FRR keeps running. The **Router ID** can only be filled in while an AS number is set, and clearing the AS clears it too.", "**Announce only.** The node announces its anycast addresses and nothing else, and accepts no routes from its neighbors, so a peer cannot change this host's routing table.", "**Withdrawal.** An address exists in FRR's table only while AnyTime holds it on `lo` (an NTP server answers, the gateway is not paused, AnyTime is running), so the route is withdrawn when the node cannot answer. **BFD** is on for every neighbor; lower the timers on your router to shorten the time it takes to notice a dead node.", "An **IPv4** address is announced to IPv4 neighbors and an **IPv6** address to IPv6 neighbors; add a neighbor of each family to announce both.", "**Existing FRR setup.** The first time AnyTime takes `frr.conf` over, the old file is kept as `frr.conf.pre-ntpdir`. A change is applied with a reload, which needs FRR's `frr-reload.py` (on Debian and Ubuntu the `frr-pythontools` package, which the installer adds; on RPM distributions it is part of `frr`); without it AnyTime restarts FRR instead and the sessions drop briefly."]],
      ["h", "Neighbors"],
      ["ul", ["Press **+** above the table to add a row and fill it in: the neighbor is created once it has an address and an AS number. Click a row to highlight it and press **−** to remove it.", "Changes save when you leave a field. The password is an MD5 session password; it is kept in the config file and its history, readable by root only."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "These belong to this node, not the cluster. The anycast addresses themselves are shared.",
      ["fields", [["Local AS number", "This node's AS number, for example `64512`. BGP runs on this node while it is set; clear it to turn BGP off."], ["Router ID", "An IPv4 address that identifies this router. Optional; empty lets FRR pick one. Needs a local AS number: the field is disabled until one is set."], ["Keepalive (seconds)", "How often this node sends a BGP keepalive. Default `3`. Must be shorter than the hold time."], ["AS Prepend", "When ticked, this node adds its local AS three more times to the AS path of every anycast address it announces (`set as-path prepend ASN ASN ASN` in the outbound route-map). A longer path makes routers prefer the nodes that do not have it ticked, so use it on the nodes that should be the backup. Per node, like the rest of this card; it applies at once."], ["Hold time (seconds)", "How long a silent peer is kept before the session is declared down. Default `9`, minimum `3`. The session uses the lower hold time of the two ends, so a peer with longer timers agrees down to this."], ["AS of (a neighbor's row)", "The neighbor's AS number. A neighbor is created once it has an address and an AS."], ["Description of (a neighbor's row)", "A note of up to 60 characters shown with the neighbor."], ["Password for (a neighbor's row)", "Optional MD5 session password; it must match the router's. The eye beside the box shows or hides what is typed. Kept in the config file and its history, readable by root only."], ["Enabled (a neighbor's row)", "Off shuts this one session down (`neighbor … shutdown` in `frr.conf`) and nothing is announced to it; the neighbor stays configured. The same as right-click ▸ Disable under **Operate → Anycast**. Greyed out while BGP itself is off."], ["Multihop of (a neighbor's row)", "For an eBGP peer that is not on a directly connected subnet, for example an AWS VPC Route Server endpoint: the hop limit, usually `2`. Empty means directly connected. FRR then runs BFD to that peer in multihop mode as well, so the peer must be set up the same way (one side multihop and the other not leaves BFD down and the session resetting). Ignored for an iBGP neighbor."], ["New neighbor address", "The router's IPv4 or IPv6 address, for example `192.0.2.1` or `2001:db8::1`, in the empty row above the table."], ["New neighbor AS", "That router's AS number."], ["New neighbor multihop", "Optional, 2-255. See *Multihop* in a neighbor's row."], ["New neighbor description", "Optional note."], ["New neighbor password", "Optional MD5 session password."]]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --bgp\nntpdir --asn 64512 --router-id 192.0.2.10   (--router-id needs an AS, set now or earlier)\nntpdir --keepalive 3 --hold 9\nntpdir --as-prepend on   (the local AS three more times on every announcement; off undoes it)\nntpdir --bgp-neighbor-add 192.0.2.1 --remote-as 64500 --description core\nntpdir --bgp-neighbor-add 10.0.1.5 --remote-as 64512 --multihop 2\nntpdir --bgp-neighbor-del 192.0.2.1\nntpdir --asn off   (also clears the router ID)\n(--password on the command line is visible to other users of the host; the page is safer)"],
    ],
  },
  anycast_ospf: {
    title: "Configure: Anycast ▸ OSPF",
    body: [
      "Announce the gateways' **anycast addresses** to your routers with OSPF instead of (or as well as) BGP. OSPFv2 carries the IPv4 addresses and OSPFv3 the IPv6 ones. AnyTime drives FRR on this node: it writes the OSPF part of `/etc/frr/frr.conf` and reloads FRR. FRR must be installed on the node.",
      ["note", "**ospfd** and **ospf6d** (FRR's OSPFv2 and OSPFv3 programs) are switched on in `/etc/frr/daemons` **only while OSPF is set up** on this node, together with `bfdd`. When you remove the last interface or press *Disable OSPF* (Operate ▸ Anycast) they are switched off again, so they do not run when nobody uses them. FRR is restarted when that happens."],
      ["note", "If the AnyName DNS Director is installed on the same host, BGP and OSPF are **unavailable** here. AnyName manages FRR the same way and the two would overwrite each other's file. Removing AnyName lifts this."],
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Choose the network interfaces this node speaks OSPF on, and the OSPF area of each.", "Set the router ID, the hello and dead intervals and the metric.", "Protect a link with an OSPFv2 key."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: announce your anycast addresses with OSPF"],
      ["ol", ["Add the anycast addresses to the gateway on the Topology page (right-click the circle ▸ **Add anycast address**).", "Open **Configure ▸ Anycast ▸ OSPF** on each node.", "Type the **Router ID**: an IPv4 address that is unique among your routers, for example an address of this node.", "Press **+** above the interface table, type the interface that faces your router (for example `eth0`) and leave the field. The interface is created and OSPF starts.", "Set the **Area** to the one your router uses (empty is area 0, the backbone) and the **Link type**. *Point-to-point* is the usual choice for a routed link and avoids electing a designated router.", "Watch **Monitor ▸ Anycast**: a neighbor in state *Full* is a working adjacency."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: make a node the backup"],
      ["ol", ["On that node, raise the **Metric** (for example to 100).", "Your routers then prefer the nodes with the lower metric and use this one when those are gone."]],
      ["h", "What it does"],
      ["ul", ["**Per node.** The OSPF settings are not shared with the cluster, like BGP's. The anycast addresses themselves are shared.", "**On when an interface is set.** OSPF runs on this node while at least one interface is listed. Remove them all to turn it off: the OSPF part leaves `frr.conf`, `ospfd` and `ospf6d` are switched off, and the Router ID goes with them.", "**Announce only.** The node announces its anycast addresses and nothing else (they are redistributed from `lo` through a route-map that permits only those addresses), and takes no routes from OSPF into this host's routing table, so a neighbor cannot change it. The networks of the OSPF interfaces themselves are part of OSPF and are announced as usual.", "**Withdrawal.** An address exists in FRR only while AnyTime holds it on `lo`, so the route is withdrawn when the node cannot answer. **BFD** is on for every interface; lower the timers on your router to shorten the time it takes to notice a dead node.", "**Both protocols.** You can run BGP and OSPF together. An address stays on this node while **either** carries it, and its pill on Topology shows the better of the two.", "**Existing FRR setup.** The first time AnyTime takes `frr.conf` over, the old file is kept as `frr.conf.pre-ntpdir`. A change is applied with a reload, which needs FRR's `frr-reload.py`; without it FRR is restarted for every change."]],
      ["h", "Interfaces"],
      ["ul", ["Press **+** above the table, type the interface name (the list offers this node's interfaces) and leave the field: the interface is created. The Router ID must be filled in first. Click a row to highlight it and press **−** to remove it.", "Changes save when you leave a field. The key is an OSPFv2 MD5 key (up to 16 characters); OSPFv3 does not use it. It is kept in the config file and its history, readable by root only.", "Untick **Enabled** to make an interface passive: no hellos are sent, no neighbors form, and the interface stays configured."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "These belong to this node, not the cluster. The anycast addresses themselves are shared.",
      ["fields", [["Router ID", "An IPv4 address that names this router in OSPF. It must be unique among your routers. It is needed as soon as there is an interface, and is saved together with the first one."], ["Hello interval", "How often, in seconds, hellos are sent on every interface. Empty or 10 uses FRR's default. It must be the same on the router at the other end, and shorter than the dead interval."], ["Dead interval", "How many seconds without a hello before a neighbor is declared down. Empty or 40 uses FRR's default. It must be the same on the router at the other end. BFD usually notices a dead neighbor much sooner."], ["Metric", "The cost the announced anycast addresses carry (empty or 20 is FRR's default). A node with a higher metric is the less preferred one, the OSPF way to make a node the backup. The addresses are announced as external routes of type 1, so the cost of the path to this node is added."], ["Area of (an interface's row)", "The OSPF area of the interface: a number (0 is the backbone) or a dotted address such as 0.0.0.1. Empty is area 0. It must be the same as on the router at the other end."], ["Link type of (an interface's row)", "*Default* leaves it to FRR (broadcast on Ethernet). *Point-to-point* is for a link between two routers and skips the designated-router election. *Broadcast* is for a shared segment. It must match the other end."], ["Key for (an interface's row)", "The OSPFv2 MD5 key of the link, up to 16 characters. Empty means no authentication. It applies to IPv4 only; OSPFv3 runs without it."], ["Enabled (an interface's row)", "Off makes the interface passive: no hellos, no neighbors, and the addresses are no longer announced over it. The interface stays configured. The same switch is under Operate ▸ Anycast."], ["New interface name", "The name of a network interface on this node, for example `eth0`. The list offers the ones that exist."], ["New interface area", "The area of the new interface; empty is area 0."], ["New interface link type", "The link type of the new interface."], ["New interface key", "An optional OSPFv2 MD5 key for the new interface."]]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --ospf\nntpdir --ospf-router-id 192.0.2.10 --ospf-interface-add eth0   (the router id is saved with the first interface)\nntpdir --ospf-interface-add eth1 --ospf-area 0.0.0.1 --ospf-network point-to-point --ospf-key SECRET\nntpdir --ospf-hello 10 --ospf-dead 40 --ospf-metric 100   (- goes back to the default)\nntpdir --ospf-interface-del eth1   (removing the last one turns OSPF off and clears the router id)\n(--ospf-key on the command line is visible to other users of the host; the page is safer)"],
    ],
  },
  users_local: {
    title: "Users: Local",
    body: [
      "The accounts that may sign in to this web console with a user name and password. They are the members of the console's group (`ntpdir`, unless **Login groups** on Configure ▸ Web GUI says otherwise; the first of its groups that is local). They are ordinary operating-system accounts, so a password set here is the one PAM checks.",
      "In a cluster every change here is made on **every node**. The password hash is sent, not the password. If a node could not be reached the message says which, and the change can be repeated when it is back.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Add an account, or put an existing account on the machine into the console's group.", "Change a password or an expiry date.", "Disable an account (it keeps existing but cannot sign in here), or delete it.", "Give someone read-only access."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: add a person"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Users ▸ Local** and press **+ New**.", "Type the **Name** and a **Password**. Optionally set **Expires**. Choose the person's access: full or **Read-only**.", "Press **Add user**. The account has no shell and no home directory, so it can sign in here and do nothing else on the machine."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: change a password or an expiry date"],
      ["ol", ["Pick the account in the list on the left.", "Type a **New password** and/or change **Expires**. A blank date means the account never expires. From the date on the operating system refuses the account.", "Press **Save** (Enter in the password field does the same)."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: let an account that already exists sign in here"],
      ["ol", ["Press **Add an existing account…**.", "Type its name in **Name**. The box suggests ordinary accounts that are not in the group yet.", "Nothing about the account is changed except that it joins the console's group. It keeps the password it already has. On the other nodes it is added to the group where it exists, and created with the same password where it does not."]],
      ["h", "Disable or delete"],
      ["ul", ["**Disable** takes the account out of the group and keeps the account. It works for any listed account except yourself and the last one. An account that is in the group only because it is its primary group is refused, with the reason.", "**Delete** removes the account. You cannot delete yourself or the last account that can sign in."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      ["fields", [["Name", "Optional label shown instead of the address. Shared."], ["Password", "On the sign-in page: that user's password, checked through PAM. After too many wrong attempts the sign-in page is locked for a while."], ["Expires", "Optional date after which the account cannot sign in. Empty means never."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["Only accounts in the console's group are listed, and `root` never is. *Add user* never changes an account that already exists on a machine; use *Add an existing account…* for that.", "A node that was down misses a change. Setting the password again sends the whole account again (and creates it where it is missing), which brings it back in line. `ntpdir --user-del NAME` can repeat a delete.", "A node that **joins** a cluster gets its existing accounts copied (with their passwords and expiry). An account that already exists on the new node is left as it is.", "Every member can manage every other member, which is the same power they already have over the daemon. Changes are logged with who made them.", "Changing a user's password, deleting the user or letting the account expire signs that user out of every open session."]],
      ["h", "Active Directory through sssd"],
      "Directory accounts (Active Directory, through sssd) can sign in without being created here. Do this on every node; the README has the full steps (*Signing in with Active Directory*).",
      ["ul", ["**Join the node**: `realm join -U Administrator example.com` (with `realmd`, `sssd`, `adcli` installed; the node needs DNS for the domain and a clock within 5 minutes of the controller). In the domain's section of `/etc/sssd/sssd.conf` set `use_fully_qualified_names = False`, `fallback_homedir = /nonexistent` and `default_shell = /sbin/nologin`, then `systemctl restart sssd`.", "**Make a group in the directory** called `ntpdir-users` and put the people in it. Its name must differ from the local `ntpdir` group: the local group is looked up first and would hide a directory group of the same name.", "**Login groups** (Configure ▸ Web) takes both, separated by a comma: `ntpdir,ntpdir-users`. That is the default for a new installation; an existing configuration is not changed by an update, so set it on each node. A member of either may sign in; a group that does not exist on a node is skipped. This page manages the first one that is local.", "**Group policy**: sssd refuses a PAM service it has no logon right for, which gives `pam account check (password accepted): Permission denied` in the log although ssh works. The daemon adds `ad_gpo_map_interactive = +ntpdir` to the domain's section of `sssd.conf` itself when it starts (the old file is kept as `sssd.conf.ntpdir.bak`) and restarts sssd.", "**In a container** (LXC, Proxmox) only the IDs of its `uid_map` exist (see `/proc/self/uid_map`), and sssd makes AD users' IDs from 200000 up, so the sign-in fails. The daemon then sets `ldap_idmap_range_min/max/size = 10000/60000/50000` in the domain's section of `sssd.conf` at start (unless the domain has a range of its own), clears sssd's cache and restarts it. Use the same range on every node.", "**Check** with `sss_cache -E`, `id NAME` (lists `ntpdir-users`), `getent group ntpdir-users` and `pamtester ntpdir NAME authenticate acct_mgmt`. Sign in with the name `id` prints. The reason for a refusal is in the log: `journalctl -u ntpdir | grep 'login failed'`.", "A directory account keeps its password and expiry in the directory, so those buttons refuse it here. *Add an existing account…* can still add one by name to the local group instead of using a directory group."]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --users\nntpdir --user-add NAME [--expires YYYY-MM-DD]\nntpdir --user-passwd NAME\nntpdir --user-expiry NAME --expires YYYY-MM-DD|never\nntpdir --user-del NAME\nntpdir --user-grant NAME\nntpdir --user-revoke NAME"],
    ],
  },
  users_sso0: {
    title: "Users: SSO ▸ General",
    body: [
      "Single sign-on lets people sign in with a Google or Microsoft (Entra ID) account, or with an account at any other OpenID Connect provider you run yourself (Authentik, Keycloak, Authelia, Zitadel), instead of a system password. **The password login stays, so you cannot lock yourself out.**",
      "A button for each provider you set up (**Sign in with Google**, **Sign in with Microsoft**, and a button with the name you chose for OpenID Connect) then appears on the sign-in page.",
      "This tab holds the table of **people who may use single sign-on** and the address of this console. The provider settings are on the **Google**, **Microsoft** and **OpenID Connect** tabs next to it.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Choose who may sign in with single sign-on, and with what access.", "Say which provider each person must use.", "Set the address the provider sends people back to."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: let someone sign in with single sign-on"],
      ["ol", ["Set up at least one provider on its own tab (Google, Microsoft or OpenID Connect). A provider is offered only when all of its fields are filled in.", "Back on **General**, add a row to **Users**: the person's **E-mail address**.", "Choose the **Access**: `rw` (may change settings) or `ro` (may only look). A new row starts as `ro`.", "Optionally choose the **Provider**: `any` (the default) or the one provider this address may use.", "Leave the field. The person can sign in now."]],
      ["h", "How it works"],
      ["ul", ["Only the e-mail addresses in the **Users** table can sign in, whatever account they use at the provider. Anybody else is turned away even with a valid Google or Microsoft account.", "The console checks the provider's signed ID token (signature, issuer, audience, expiry and a one-time nonce; PKCE is used). Google addresses must be verified by Google. Microsoft accounts must belong to the configured tenant.", "An SSO session is checked against the table every minute: removing an address ends it, and changing the level changes it.", "Addresses are not case sensitive.", "The settings are shared by the cluster. Each node needs HTTPS access to the provider (accounts.google.com, login.microsoftonline.com or your own provider)."]],
      ["h", "Which provider an address uses"],
      "Each row of the **Users** table has a **Provider** column. **any** (the default) lets the address sign in through any provider that is set up and vouches for it. **google**, **microsoft** or **oidc** admits it through that provider only, so an address that someone could also register at another provider cannot be used there. The log says which provider each sign-in used (`web: login: \"alice@example.com\" signed in with Google from ... (full access)`).",
      ["h", "The redirect address"],
      "Every provider has to be told where to send people back to after they sign in. That address is `https://THIS-NODE:12380/api/sso/callback`: the address in your browser's bar, then `/api/sso/callback`. In a cluster, register each node's address, or fill in **Address of this console** so every node uses one.",
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["Address of this console", "Optional, `https://name:port` with no path. The provider sends the browser back to this address plus `/api/sso/callback`, so register exactly that with the provider. Empty uses the address the browser used to reach this node. Shared."], ["Users", "Who may sign in with single sign-on, as a table: the **E-mail address** and the **Access**, `rw` (may change settings) or `ro` (may only look); a new row starts as `ro`. **Provider** is `any` (the default) or the one provider the address may use: `google`, `microsoft` or `oidc`. Rows can be edited, dragged, filtered and right-clicked like the zone table. Addresses are not case sensitive. Up to 5000. Nobody else gets in through a provider, even with a valid account there. Shared."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["The **Users** table works like the other tables: rows can be edited, dragged, filtered and right-clicked (Edit, Copy, Paste, Add row, Move, Delete). Up to 5000 rows.", "The client secrets are stored in the configuration file like the other secrets and are not shown to read-only accounts.", "The log says which provider each sign-in used (`web: login: \"alice@example.com\" signed in with Google from ... (full access)`)."]],
      "The keys are in the `web` block: `sso_base_url`, `sso_google_client_id`, `sso_google_client_secret`, `sso_entra_tenant`, `sso_entra_client_id`, `sso_entra_client_secret`, `sso_oidc_name`, `sso_oidc_issuer`, `sso_oidc_client_id`, `sso_oidc_client_secret` and `sso_users` (one `alice@example.com rw` or `bob@example.com ro google` per entry; no level means `ro`, no provider means any). There is no separate command line for them; use `--config-import`.",
    ],
  },
  users_sso1: {
    title: "Users: SSO ▸ Google",
    body: [
      "Lets people sign in with a Google account. You register this console with Google once, then paste the two values Google gives you here.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Turn on **Sign in with Google** on the sign-in page.", "Keep the Google client ID and secret."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: set up Google sign-in"],
      ["ol", ["In Google Cloud Console go to **APIs & Services ▸ Credentials ▸ Create credentials ▸ OAuth client ID**, and choose **Web application**.", "Add the redirect URI `https://THIS-NODE:12380/api/sso/callback`: the address in your browser's bar, then `/api/sso/callback`. In a cluster register each node's, or set **Address of this console** on the General tab.", "Copy the client ID and the client secret into the two fields below.", "Go to the **General** tab and add the e-mail addresses that may sign in. Only they get in."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["Google client ID", "The OAuth client ID of the Google web application (ends in `.apps.googleusercontent.com`). Google is offered only when both the ID and the secret are filled in. Only addresses Google has verified are accepted. Shared."], ["Google client secret", "The OAuth client secret of the Google web application. Stored in the configuration file like the other secrets, and not shown to read-only accounts. Shared."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["Google is offered on the sign-in page only when **both** fields are filled in.", "Only addresses Google has verified are accepted.", "The secret is kept in the configuration file and is not shown to read-only accounts."]],
    ],
  },
  users_sso2: {
    title: "Users: SSO ▸ Microsoft",
    body: [
      "Lets people sign in with a Microsoft (Entra ID) account of your organisation. You register this console in Entra once, then copy three values here.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Turn on **Sign in with Microsoft** on the sign-in page.", "Keep the tenant ID, the client ID and the client secret."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: set up Microsoft sign-in"],
      ["ol", ["In Microsoft Entra go to **App registrations ▸ New registration**. Choose the platform **Web**, with the redirect URI `https://THIS-NODE:12380/api/sso/callback`.", "From the app's overview copy the **Directory (tenant) ID** and the **Application (client) ID**.", "Under **Certificates & secrets ▸ New client secret** make a secret and copy its **Value** (not its ID).", "Paste the three values into the fields below.", "Go to the **General** tab and add the e-mail addresses that may sign in."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["Microsoft tenant ID", "The Directory (tenant) ID of your Microsoft Entra organisation, a GUID like `11111111-2222-3333-4444-555555555555`. Only accounts of this tenant can sign in (the token's issuer and tenant must match); `common` and domain names are not accepted. Microsoft is offered only when the tenant, client ID and secret are all filled in. The address is taken from the `email` claim, or from `preferred_username` when there is none. Shared."], ["Microsoft client ID", "The Application (client) ID of the app registration. Shared."], ["Microsoft client secret", "The client secret's **Value** (not its ID) from Certificates & secrets. Microsoft secrets expire: set a reminder to make a new one. Not shown to read-only accounts. Shared."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["**Microsoft secrets expire.** Set a reminder to make a new one before it does.", "Microsoft is offered on the sign-in page only when the tenant, client ID and secret are all filled in.", "Only accounts of this tenant can sign in (the token's issuer and tenant must match). `common` and domain names are not accepted.", "The address is taken from the `email` claim, or from `preferred_username` when there is none."]],
    ],
  },
  users_sso3: {
    title: "Users: SSO ▸ OpenID Connect",
    body: [
      "Lets people sign in with an account at your own identity provider, such as Authentik, Keycloak, Authelia or Zitadel. The provider appears on the sign-in page as a button with the name you give it.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Turn on a sign-in button for your own provider.", "Choose the button's name, and whether the provider's certificate is checked."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: set up your own provider"],
      ["ol", ["In your provider, create an OpenID Connect client: a *confidential* client using the authorization code flow, with the redirect URI `https://THIS-NODE:12380/api/sso/callback`. The scopes `openid`, `email` and `profile` are used.", "Set the provider to sign ID tokens with **RS256** (an RSA key). Tokens signed another way, including the HMAC some providers use when no signing key is chosen, are refused.", "Mark the user's e-mail as **verified** in the provider. The address is taken from the `email` claim and the provider must say it is verified (`email_verified`).", "Fill in the issuer, the client ID and the client secret below, and give the button a name (for example *Company SSO*).", "Go to the **General** tab and add the e-mail addresses that may sign in."]],
      ["h", "Finding the issuer"],
      "The **issuer** is the address in the provider's discovery document, the part before `/.well-known/openid-configuration`. Examples: `https://auth.example.lan/application/o/ntpdir/` (Authentik), `https://auth.example.lan/realms/main` (Keycloak). It must be https, and the document must name that same issuer.",
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["OpenID Connect button name", "The text of the extra button on the login page, for your own OpenID Connect provider: *Sign in with* and this name. Up to 40 characters; empty is *SSO*. Shared."], ["OpenID Connect issuer", "The provider's issuer address, `https` and no path beyond the provider's own, for example `https://auth.example.lan/realms/main`. A trailing slash is ignored. The console reads `/.well-known/openid-configuration` below it. Shared."], ["OpenID Connect client ID", "The client ID of the confidential client registered with the provider. Shared."], ["OpenID Connect client secret", "The client's secret. Stored in the configuration file like the other secrets and not shown to read-only accounts. The provider is offered only when issuer, client ID and secret are filled in. Shared."], ["Do not check the provider's certificate", "For your own OpenID Connect provider only. On: the console accepts any certificate the provider presents, a self-signed one for instance, so the host does not have to trust your certificate authority. The connection is still encrypted, but nothing proves who is on the other end: somebody who can put a machine between this node and the provider could read the sign-in traffic and impersonate the provider, which would let them sign in as anyone on the Users list. Use it for a test or a network you trust; the log warns about it (at most once an hour). Off by default. Shared."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["The provider is offered only when the issuer, client ID and client secret are all filled in. An empty button name shows *SSO*.", "If the provider uses a certificate from your own certificate authority, that authority must be trusted by this host's system certificate store. Or tick *Do not check the provider's certificate* (less safe).", "That option keeps the connection encrypted but proves nothing about who is at the other end. Somebody who can put a machine between this node and the provider could read the sign-in traffic and impersonate the provider, which would let them sign in as anyone on the Users list. Use it for a test or a network you trust. The log warns about it (at most once an hour)."]],
    ],
  },
  users_ldap: {
    title: "Users: LDAP",
    body: [
      "Lets people sign in with an account from a directory, an Active Directory domain or a Samba 4 domain, using the ordinary user name and password form of the sign-in page. The host's own accounts are tried first; when they do not accept the name and password, the directory is asked. **The system login keeps working, so you cannot lock yourself out.**",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Point the console at your directory servers.", "Choose which directory groups get full access and which read-only.", "Try the settings with the **Test** box before anyone depends on them."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: set it up (recommended, with a bind account)"],
      ["ol", ["In your directory make an account just for this, with no special rights. It only needs to read users and groups. With Samba: `samba-tool user create svc-console` (give it a long password, and `samba-tool user setexpiry svc-console --noexpiry` so it does not expire).", "Make the groups and add people: `samba-tool group add console-admins`, `samba-tool group add console-readers`, `samba-tool group addmembers console-admins alice`.", "Open **Users ▸ LDAP**. Add your server(s) to **LDAP servers**, for example `ldaps://dc1.example.lan:636`.", "Fill in **Search base** (the top of the domain, like `DC=example,DC=lan`), **Bind account** and **Bind password**, **Full-access group** and **Read-only group**. Groups can be a name or a full DN.", "Press **Save**, then use **Test** with a user name and password. It lists each step."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: set it up without a bind account"],
      ["ol", ["Leave **Bind account** and **Bind password** empty.", "Fill in the servers, the search base and the groups as above.", "Each person now signs in to the directory as themselves. A name typed as `alice` is sent as `alice@example.lan`, the domain taken from the search base. `alice@example.lan` and `EXAMPLE\\alice` are sent as typed."]],
      ["note", "Without a bind account a session cannot be rechecked. Removing somebody from the group or disabling the account stops new sign-ins, but their open session runs until it expires or they sign out. Use a bind account when you need sessions to end at once."],
      ["h", "How it works"],
      ["ul", ["The console connects to a directory server over TLS (`ldaps://`, or `ldap://` with StartTLS: it never sends a password without encryption, and if StartTLS is refused it stops before sending anything). With a **bind account** it binds as that account, finds the person by `sAMAccountName` or by `userPrincipalName` (`alice`, `EXAMPLE\\alice` and `alice@example.lan` all work), checks that the account is enabled, and checks that it is a member of a group below. Only then is the password checked, by signing in to the directory as that person. (Without a bind account see the next point.)", "**Without a bind account** (leave the bind account and password empty) each person signs in to the directory as themselves, and the console reads their own groups and account status. A name typed as `alice` is sent to the directory as `alice@example.lan`, the domain taken from the search base (`DC=example,DC=lan`); `alice@example.lan` and `EXAMPLE\\alice` are sent as typed. This needs nothing set up in the directory, but a session cannot be rechecked: removing somebody from the group or disabling the account stops new sign-ins, and their open session runs until it expires or they sign out. Use the bind account when you need that.", "Membership counts through nested groups too (a group inside a group), the way Active Directory's own tools see it.", "Access comes from the groups: members of the **Full-access group** may change settings, members of the **Read-only group** may only look. Somebody in both has full access. An account in neither is refused.", "With a bind account, a session started this way is checked against the directory every minute, so disabling the account or taking it out of the group ends the session. If the directory cannot be reached for that check the session simply stays until it can be.", "Computer accounts are never signed in, a name that matches more than one account signs nobody in, and an empty password is never sent. The name typed in is used as a value in the search, never as part of its text, so it cannot change what is asked.", "Wrong passwords count toward the same lockout as every other login.", "The settings are shared by the cluster. The bind password is stored in the configuration file like the other secrets and is not shown to read-only accounts."]],
      ["h", "Samba and Active Directory"],
      ["ul", ["The **search base** is the top of the domain, like `DC=example,DC=lan` for `example.lan`. The **bind account** is its DN (`CN=svc-console,CN=Users,DC=example,DC=lan`) or its user principal name (`svc-console@example.lan`).", "Use port 636 (`ldaps://dc1.example.lan:636`). Samba makes itself a self-signed certificate (`tls.crt` under its `private/tls` folder): either make this host trust that certificate's authority, or tick *Do not check the server's certificate* (less safe, see below).", "List more than one server and they are tried in order; the first that answers is used."]],
      ["h", "The Test box"],
      "The Test box uses the settings as last saved, so press Save first after changing anything. A user name alone checks the lookup and the groups; a password as well also checks the password. It lists what happened step by step, such as which server answered, whether the bind account was accepted, which account was found, and whether it is in each group. A person's password typed here is sent to the console and used once for the check, and is not kept.",
      "With nothing typed, **Test** just connects and signs in the bind account. Without a bind account a person can only be tried with their password.",
      ["h", "Fields"],
      "Each setting below says **Shared** or **This node only**. A *shared* setting is the same on every node: change it on any node and the cluster copies it everywhere. A setting marked *this node only* belongs to the node you are working on.",
      ["fields", [["LDAP servers", "The directory servers, one address per row: `ldaps://dc1.example.lan:636` (TLS from the start, port 636 unless written) or `ldap://dc1.example.lan:389` (StartTLS is required). Up to 8, tried in order. Shared."], ["Do not check the server's certificate", "On: any certificate the server presents is accepted, a self-signed one for instance, so the host does not have to trust your certificate authority. The connection is still encrypted, but nothing proves who is on the other end: somebody who can put a machine between this node and the directory could read every password typed here and answer as the directory. Use it for a test or a network you trust; the log warns about it (at most once an hour). Off by default. Shared."], ["Bind account", "Optional. The account the console looks people up with: a DN (`CN=svc-console,CN=Users,DC=example,DC=lan`) or a user principal name (`svc-console@example.lan`). It needs no rights beyond reading users and groups. Leave it, and the password, empty to have each person sign in as themselves (see above). Shared."], ["Bind password", "That account's password. Stored in the configuration file and not shown to read-only accounts. LDAP sign-in is on when servers, search base and at least one group are filled in, and the bind account and password are either both filled in or both empty. Shared."], ["Search base", "Where people and groups are searched, including everything below it: `DC=example,DC=lan`. Shared."], ["Full-access group", "A group (its name, like `console-admins`, or its full DN) whose members may change settings. Nested groups count. Shared."], ["Read-only group", "A group (name or DN) whose members may only look. Somebody in this group and the full-access group has full access. Shared."]]],
      "The keys are in the `web` block: `ldap_servers` (a list), `ldap_skip_verify`, `ldap_bind_dn`, `ldap_bind_password`, `ldap_base_dn`, `ldap_rw_group` and `ldap_ro_group`. There is no separate command line for them; use `--config-import`.",
    ],
  },
  history: {
    title: "History",
    body: [
      "Every change to the configuration is recorded: from the web console, the command line, a cluster sync or a file edit. Each version shows who made it and a plain summary of what changed. Secrets are hidden in the summaries. The newest 200 versions are kept.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["See what changed, when, and who changed it.", "Compare two versions.", "Go back to an older version.", "Save the configuration to a file, or load one from a file."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: see what a change did"],
      ["ol", ["Click a version in the list. It is highlighted, and the buttons in the list's header act on that one.", "Press **Diff vs previous** to compare it with the version before it, or **Diff vs live** to compare it with what is running now.", "Press **View** to see the whole version."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: compare any two versions"],
      ["ol", ["Tick the box on two versions (**Compare version**).", "Press **Compare ticked**."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: go back to an older version"],
      ["ol", ["Select the version and press **Restore**.", "It applies at once. The current configuration is saved as a new version first, so you can always come back."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: save or load a configuration file"],
      ["ol", ["**Download** saves the selected version as a file. **Snapshot now** records the current state as a version of its own.", "**Upload** asks for a configuration `.json` file (**Configuration file**), such as one saved with Download. It is checked first, and applied only if it is valid."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      ["fields", [["Compare version", "The tick box on each version. Tick two and choose **Compare ticked** to see what differs between them."], ["Configuration file", "What **Upload** asks for: a configuration `.json` file, such as one saved with **Download**. It is checked first and applied only if valid."]]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --versions\nntpdir --version-show ID\nntpdir --version-diff A[..B]\nntpdir --version-snapshot [--note TEXT]   (a note can only be given here)\nntpdir --version-restore ID\nntpdir --version-export [ID]\nntpdir --config-import FILE"],
    ],
  },
  cluster: {
    title: "Operate: Cluster",
    body: [
      "A cluster keeps the settings of several AnyTime nodes in step, and lets you update them together. Every node starts as the primary of a cluster of one until it joins another. Clustering is separate from the gateway election, which works the same with or without it.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Add a node to the cluster, or take one out.", "Make a replica the primary when the old primary is gone.", "See whether every node is reachable and running the same version.", "Pull the latest settings to this node at once (**Sync now**)."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: add a node"],
      ["ol", ["On any member of the cluster, choose **Create join code**. The code works once and for an hour, and the member shows it read-only for you to copy.", "On the new node, open **Operate ▸ Cluster** and paste the code under **Join code**. Press **Join**.", "The new node's shared settings are replaced by the cluster's. Its interface, priority and weight stay its own.", "Add the node to the gateways it should serve (Topology ▸ right-click the gateway ▸ **Add node**). A node that has just joined starts out removed from all of them."]],
      ["note", "The new node does not offer the release it had staged to the others, so joining with a newer version never pulls the cluster up to it. A joining node on an **older** version still updates itself from the cluster."],
      ["h", "Step by step: make another node the primary"],
      ["ol", ["Pick the replica you want in the **Node** menu in the top right.", "Choose **Promote**."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: remove a node"],
      ["ol", ["Right-click the member (not this node) and choose **Delete**.", "The member is dropped for good and nothing is kept about it. A copy that is still running resets itself to a cluster of one. Its address can be used by a new node.", "From the node itself, **Leave cluster** does the same."]],
      ["h", "Roles and membership"],
      ["ul", ["One node is the **primary**; the others are **replicas**. Shared settings are checked on the primary and copied out within the sync interval.", "If the primary is gone for good, choose **Promote** on the replica you want. Failover is automatic: a replica promotes itself when the primary has not answered for 5 minutes and more than half of the members, that replica included, agree; the one with the newest settings promotes and the others follow. It is on from three members and off with two. A replica cut off from the others never has a majority, so a network split cannot create two primaries; with two members only you can promote.", "Right-click a member (not this node) and choose **Delete**: the member is dropped for good, nothing is kept about it (a copy that is still running resets itself to a cluster of one), and it is not put on a removed list, so a new node can use its address. Command line: `--cluster-delete ADDR`. **Leave cluster** does it from the node itself. (`--cluster-remove` still drops a member but blocks its address and identity until `--cluster-unremove`, for a node that is still running and must not come back.)", "**Sync now** pulls the latest state immediately."]],
      ["h", "The epoch"],
      "The epoch is a counter that says which node is the primary. It starts at 1 and goes up by one each time a node becomes primary: when you **Promote** it, or when the primary is restarted, shut down (now, or a minute before a scheduled one) or updated on purpose and hands the role to the replica with the newest settings (it comes back as a replica, and the role stays where it landed). A primary that goes offline unexpectedly keeps the role until you promote another node or, with Failover on, the cluster does after 5 minutes; joins, syncs and members going offline never change it. Every member follows the primary with the highest epoch, so an old primary that comes back still claiming a lower epoch is ignored and falls in line as a replica. If two different nodes ever claim the same epoch (two admins promoting on either side of a network split), the nodes refuse the conflict instead of picking one. An epoch of 1 everywhere means nothing has been promoted yet.",
      ["h", "The Members table"],
      ["ul", ["**Reachable**: whether this node could ask that member for its status just now; when it says no, the reason is shown.", "**Epoch**: that member's view of the epoch (see above). They should all agree.", "**Running** and **Source**: the version the member is running, and the newest uploaded source version it holds.", "**Clock vs this node (ms)**: how far that member's clock is from this node's clock. Every node measures its clock against the same servers (`chronyc tracking`) and tells the others, and the difference of the two readings is the difference of the clocks, accurate to about the error of chrony's own estimate (usually well under a millisecond on a LAN; hover a figure for both readings). *this node* marks your row, *not synchronised* a member whose chrony has no time yet, and – a member that does not say (an older version, chrony not running, or not reachable). Under the table, **Largest difference between nodes** is the gap between the two members furthest apart. A node whose gap grows toward **Serve only within** (Configure ▸ NTP ▸ Health) is about to leave service.", "**Last seen**: when the member last answered."]],
      ["h", "Working on another member"],
      "Every page follows the **Node** menu in the top right, this one included. Pick a member, and Leave, Promote, Create join code and Sync act on it.",
      ["h", "Fields"],
      ["fields", [["Node", "The menu at the top right of every page. It picks which member the page shows and acts on: this node (the default) or any other member of the cluster."], ["Join code", "On the new node: paste the code made by **Create join code** on a member. It is single-use, valid for an hour, and not shown again; the member shows it read only for you to copy."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["Nodes pin each other's certificates and sign every request with the cluster secret.", "The cluster port (12381 by default) is separate from the console port and must be reachable between the nodes."]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --cluster-status\nntpdir --cluster-token\nntpdir --cluster-join CODE\nntpdir --cluster-promote\nntpdir --cluster-delete ADDR / --cluster-remove ADDR / --cluster-unremove ADDR\nntpdir --cluster-leave\nntpdir --cluster-sync"],
    ],
  },
  updates: {
    title: "Operate: Upgrade",
    body: [
      "Update AnyTime in place. You give one node a release archive, and the other nodes copy it from there. Each node then builds and installs it, one at a time, and rolls back by itself if the new version does not stay up.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Upload a release archive (`ntpdir_vN.tgz` or `.zip`), or fetch the newest one from the internet.", "Start the update by hand, on this node or on chosen nodes.", "Watch each node's version, whether it is queued or updating, and the history of every update."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: update with automatic updating on (the default)"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Operate ▸ Upgrade** on any node.", "Choose the release archive, or tick **Fetch from online**, and press **Upload**.", "The release is checked and kept as the **source**. The other nodes pull it from there, and every node installs it by itself, one at a time.", "Watch the table: it says why a node is waiting. The first node is the canary; the rest wait until it has run the new version for 2 minutes with no bad gateway."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: update by hand"],
      ["ol", ["Turn off **Update every node automatically…** on **Configure ▸ Settings ▸ Upgrades**.", "Upload the release as above.", "Press **Update this node now**. Or tick one or more members (**Select**) and press **Update N selected**; those members update one at a time."]],
      ["h", "How updating works"],
      ["ul", ["**Update this node now** (top right of the Nodes card) installs the source here. In a cluster, tick one or more members first and the button becomes **Update N selected**: those members update one at a time.", "While auto-update is on, the button and the tick boxes are disabled; turn it off to update by hand.", "**Auto-update** (Configure ▸ General ▸ Upgrade) (on by default) makes every member update itself whenever a newer source exists. Withdrawing a queued update (`--update-cancel`) is command line only.", "Members update **one at a time**, and the first one is the canary: the others wait until it has run the new version for 2 minutes with no bad gateway, and wait while an update has failed on any member. The table says why, and the canary shows an hourglass next to its name (hover it for the seconds left). A node that stops answering while it installs an update shows as “restarting” (Topology: grey “updating”), not as down, and its gateway is grey, not red, until it is back. A node only takes itself down when another member has been serving every gateway it serves for at least 15 seconds, so the gateway is always answered by someone and clients have moved back after the previous node's restart; otherwise it waits and the table says why. “Update now” on a node that is not safe to take down asks you to confirm before forcing it.", "A new version that does not come up is rolled back automatically."]],
      "The table (refreshed every 2 seconds) shows each member's version, source, whether it is queued or updating, and failures. The history lists every update event (the newest 500 are kept), newest first, 25 to a page: use **‹ Newer** and **Older ›** to move through them. Use the **Node** menu in the top right to work on another member.",
      ["h", "Fields"],
      ["fields", [["Release archive", "Select a `ntpdir_vN.tgz` (or `.zip`) release archive. It is checked and staged on this node; the other members pull it from here."], ["Fetch from online", "Tick it and **Upload** downloads the newest release of the online repository (Configure ▸ General ▸ Upgrade; `micush/ntpdir` unless you changed it) from GitHub (or, when it has no releases, its highest `v<number>` tag) to this node and stages it, instead of using the file you chose. This node needs to reach api.github.com and github.com (a proxy set in its environment is used). The rest is the same as an upload: the version must be newer than the one running before it can be applied."], ["Select (tick box on each member row)", "Ticks a member for **Update N selected**. Disabled while auto-update is on (Configure ▸ General)."]]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["Each node builds the daemon itself, so it needs a Go toolchain, gcc and the PAM headers. The installer sets these up.", "A node that has just joined a cluster does not offer the release it had staged, so joining with a newer version never pulls the cluster up to it. Upload or fetch a release on that node to offer it. A joining node on an older version still updates itself from the cluster.", "The version in the archive must be newer than the one running before it can be applied.", "Withdrawing a queued update (`--update-cancel`) is command line only."]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --update-status   (the 50 newest history events)\nntpdir --update-history  (all of them)\nntpdir --update-upload FILE\nntpdir --update-apply\nntpdir --update-push all|ADDR,...\nntpdir --update-cancel all|ADDR,...\nntpdir --update-auto on|off\nntpdir --update-repo URL|default"],
    ],
  },
  anycastop_bgp: {
    title: "Operate: Anycast ▸ BGP",
    body: [
      "Switch BGP on this node, or one of its neighbors, off and on without losing any setting. This page has two tabs: **BGP** (this one) and **OSPF**. The settings themselves are under **Configure ▸ Anycast ▸ BGP**; what BGP is doing is under **Monitor ▸ Anycast ▸ BGP**. The tab needs a local AS number to be set.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Stop announcing with BGP from this node (for maintenance) and start again.", "Shut down one session to one router and bring it back."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: stop announcing from this node"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Operate ▸ Anycast ▸ BGP**.", "Press **Disable BGP**. It stops BGP on this node as if the AS were cleared (the BGP section leaves `frr.conf`, the sessions go down, nothing is announced by BGP) but keeps the AS, router ID, timers and neighbors. The anycast addresses stay on `lo` while OSPF can still carry them; otherwise they are taken off and the gateways stop answering on them.", "Press **Enable BGP** to put it all back. The AS, router ID, timers and neighbors were kept."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: shut down one session"],
      ["ol", ["Right-click the neighbor and choose **Disable**. The session is shut down, nothing is announced to it, and the neighbor stays configured.", "Choose **Enable** from the same menu to bring it back."]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["A neighbor cannot be switched while BGP itself is disabled.", "It is per node, like the rest of the BGP settings. To switch another node, pick it in the **Node** menu at the top right.", "An address leaves this node only when **neither** BGP nor OSPF can carry it."]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --bgp-disable\nntpdir --bgp-enable\nntpdir --bgp-neighbor-disable 192.0.2.1\nntpdir --bgp-neighbor-enable 192.0.2.1"],
    ],
  },
  anycastop_ospf: {
    title: "Operate: Anycast ▸ OSPF",
    body: [
      "Switch OSPF on this node, or one of its interfaces, off and on without losing any setting. The settings themselves are under **Configure ▸ Anycast ▸ OSPF**; what OSPF is doing is under **Monitor ▸ Anycast ▸ OSPF**. The tab needs at least one interface to be set up.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Stop announcing with OSPF from this node (for maintenance) and start again.", "Take one interface out of service and bring it back."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: stop OSPF on this node"],
      ["ol", ["Open **Operate ▸ Anycast ▸ OSPF**.", "Press **Disable OSPF**. The OSPF part leaves `frr.conf`, `ospfd` and `ospf6d` are switched off, the neighbors go down and nothing is announced by OSPF. The router ID, interfaces and timers are kept. BGP, if it is set up, carries on.", "Press **Enable OSPF** to put it all back; `ospfd` and `ospf6d` are switched on again."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: take one interface out of service"],
      ["ol", ["Right-click the interface and choose **Disable**. It becomes passive: no hellos are sent, no neighbors form, and the interface stays configured.", "Choose **Enable** from the same menu to bring it back."]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["An interface cannot be switched while OSPF itself is disabled.", "It is per node, like the rest of the OSPF settings. To switch another node, pick it in the **Node** menu at the top right.", "An address leaves this node only when **neither** BGP nor OSPF can carry it."]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --ospf-disable   (--ospf-enable puts it back)\nntpdir --ospf-interface-disable eth0   (--ospf-interface-enable puts it back)"],
    ],
  },
  scheduler: {
    title: "Operate: Scheduler",
    body: [
      "Tasks that run by themselves, on every node of the cluster or on the nodes you choose: restart chrony, pause or resume a node, clear the statistics, restart the host, update from online, or run a shell command.",
      "The list of tasks is shared by the cluster, so the page looks the same through any node. What ran is kept per node, and shown for the node picked at the top right.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Add a task and say when it runs.", "Run a task now, on this node or on all nodes.", "Edit, copy, disable or delete a task.", "Read what ran, whether it worked, and the output of a command."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: add a task"],
      ["ol", ["Press **New task**.", "Give it a name and choose the **action**.", "Choose **when** (once, hourly, daily, weekly, monthly or yearly) and the time of day. Times are in UTC.", "Optionally set **To**, to give a window: each node then runs the task at a time of its own between the two, so they do not all act at once.", "Choose **Runs on**: every node, or the nodes you tick.", "Save. A task added or switched on starts from then; it does not run for times before."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: run a task now"],
      ["ol", ["Right-click the task and choose **Run now on…**.", "Pick **this node**, or **all nodes** (every node the task is for, started 1 minute apart, each when it is free)."]],
      ["h", "The page"],
      ["ul", ["**New task** opens the form; **right-click** a task for *Edit…*, *Copy*, *Paste*, *Run now on…* (*this node*, or *all nodes*: every node the task is for, started 1 minute apart and each when it is free), *Disable* / *Enable* and *Delete*. *Copy* remembers the task in this browser; *Paste* (in the menu, or on the button, or after right-clicking the empty list) adds a copy of it with a new name, which you can then edit. Double-click a task to edit it.", "**Next run (this node)** is when this node will run the task: the scheduled time plus this node's own place in the window (hover it for the scheduled time). The times are shown in UTC.", "**History of this node** lists what ran here, whether it worked, and the output of a command (click it to read it all). The last 300 runs are kept."]],
      ["h", "When a task runs"],
      ["ul", ["**Once**: one time, on the date you pick; **Hourly**: at a minute past the hour; **Daily**: at a time; **Weekly**: on the days you tick; **Monthly**: on a day of the month (the 31st means the last day in a shorter month, *last day* always the last), or on the *1st to 4th or last* occurrence of the weekdays you tick, such as the 3rd Saturday. When several are ticked (the 2nd and 3rd Saturday and Sunday) it runs *once* a month, on the first of those days, unless you choose *run on ▸ each of those days* (every matching day) or *one of those days, chosen per node* (each node takes one matching day of the month, the same one every time for that node and task, so the nodes do not all act on the same day; the time of day is then spread by To); **Yearly**: the same in one month. **Only in** (daily, weekly and monthly tasks) limits the task to the months you tick, such as April to October; none ticked means every month. *Every N* makes it every second, third… hour, day, week, month or year (counted from 1970 in the task's own zone, so it stays the same however often the node restarts).", "**Time of day** is UTC on every node, whatever zone the node or your browser is in. Next run, last run and the history are in UTC too; tick **Local time** at the top right of the history to see all of them in this browser's time zone (the choice is kept in this browser).", "**To** (optional) makes a window: the task is due at **At**, and each node runs it at a time of its own between **At** and **To** (At 01:00, To 10:00 means any time between 01:00 and 10:00 UTC; on an hourly task both are minutes past the hour, and the window is at most 59 minutes), chosen from the node's identity and the task, so the same node always gets the same place and the nodes do not all run it at once. Without **To** every node runs it at **At**. A node that was off and comes back inside the window still runs the task. A task saved by an older version with an offset window shows it as its **To** time. Tasks whose windows overlap are staggered on a node, so no two start less than a minute apart. Only one job runs at a time on a node: a job that falls due while another is running waits (the table says *waiting for another task*) and starts when that one has finished.", "**A node that was off or busy** runs a task it missed when it comes back, once, if that is still inside the window; older runs are skipped and the history says how many. A task that is still running when it is due again is skipped, and says so.", "**Runs on**: every node, or the nodes you tick. A task added or switched on starts from then; it does not run for times before."]],
      ["h", "Shell commands"],
      "A shell command runs with `/bin/sh -c` as root on each node, in the daemon's directory. The PATH has the usual system directories and no login profile is read. The output and the exit status are kept in the history, and the command is stopped when it runs past its time limit (the whole process group). Anyone who can sign in to this page can make a node run any command, so give the sign-in group (Operate ▸ Users) only to people you would give root.",
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["A shell command **fails** when it exits with anything but 0, or is stopped by its time limit. Tick **Ignore the exit status** for a command whose non-zero exit is an answer, such as `systemctl status` on a stopped service or `grep`: the run then counts as ok and its output ends with the exit status. A command that cannot start, or is stopped by the time limit, still fails. On the command line add `\"ignore_exit\":true` to `--schedule-set`.", "A **restart** or **reboot** waits until it is safe for the node to go down. **Update from online** installs the newest release only when it is newer than the one running.", "With auto-update on (Operate ▸ Upgrade) update from online is for one node of a cluster: the release staged there reaches the others, which update themselves. *Runs on* then offers one node only, and the server refuses the task for every node."]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --schedule   (the tasks, and when this node runs them next)\nntpdir --schedule-history   (what ran here, with the output)\nntpdir --schedule-set '{\"name\":\"3rd Saturday\",\"action\":\"chrony_restart\",\"freq\":\"monthly\",\"nths\":[2,3],\"weekdays\":[6,0],\"at\":\"01:00\",\"until\":\"10:00\"}'   (add a task; give its \"id\" to replace one; actions: chrony_restart, node_pause, node_resume, stats_clear, update_online, node_reboot, shell with \"command\")\nntpdir --schedule-del ID\nntpdir --schedule-run ID   (run it on this node now)"],
    ],
  },
  node: {
    title: "Operate: Node",
    body: [
      "What this node does, and the machine it runs on. The **Node** menu in the top right picks another member to act on.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Hand the gateway controller role to this node.", "Pause the whole node for maintenance, and resume it.", "Restart or shut down the whole machine, now or later."]],
      ["h", "Gateway controller"],
      "**Make this node the gateway controller** asks the current controller (AGC) to hand the shared address to this node. This node takes the role first and then the old controller steps down without taking its virtual MAC or its NTP service down, so clients should lose nothing; a client that cached the old controller's MAC is still answered while the new one takes that MAC over. To prefer a node permanently, raise its priority (and turn on preemption) in Settings instead.",
      ["h", "Step by step: take a node out for maintenance"],
      ["ol", ["Pick the node in the **Node** menu.", "Press **Pause this node**. It resigns, stops answering and stops probing. The other nodes carry the traffic. Nothing is shut down.", "Do your work. **Resume this node** brings its gateways back once their servers answer."]],
      "**Pause this node** pauses every gateway on it at once (the same as pausing each gateway on the Topology page, but it also covers gateways added later). The node resigns, stops answering NTP and stops probing; the other nodes carry the traffic, the gateway controller role moves to another node, and if this node was the cluster's primary the primary role is handed to a running node too. Nothing is shut down, and the setting is kept across restarts of AnyTime and of the host. **Resume this node** brings its gateways back once their NTP servers answer. It is useful for sending all traffic to one node, for example to benchmark it: pause the others instead of shutting them down. A gateway you paused yourself stays paused after the node is resumed.",
      ["h", "Step by step: restart or shut down the machine"],
      ["ol", ["Choose the **Action**: **Restart host** or **Shut down host**. It acts on the whole machine, not just ntpdir.", "Choose **When**: **Now**, **In** a number of minutes (up to 7 days) or **At** a time of day (24-hour, the host's clock).", "Press **Execute** and confirm."]],
      "A scheduled action is kept by the operating system, so it survives a restart of ntpdir. It shows here as a notice with **Cancel it**. A shut-down host stays off until it is powered on again, from its console or out-of-band.",
      ["h", "Protecting the gateway"],
      "An action started **now** is refused when this node is the only member serving one of its gateways, so a reboot never leaves clients without an answer; you are asked whether to go ahead anyway. The check is made when you press Execute, not at the scheduled time, so for a later time make sure another member will be serving by then. When a node stops, a controller announces that it is leaving, so another member takes over at once.",
      ["h", "Fields"],
      ["fields", [["Action", "**Restart host** (the default) or **Shut down host**. It acts on the whole machine."], ["When", "**Now** (default), **In** a number of minutes, or **At** a time of day. Clicking into the minutes or time box selects its option."], ["Minutes", "With **In**: 1–10080 minutes (up to 7 days), default 5."], ["Time of day", "With **At**: a 24-hour time on the host's clock; if it has passed today it means tomorrow."]]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --assert-agc\nntpdir --node-rename ntp02   (this node's host name and /etc/hosts; another node: right-click it on Topology)\nntpdir --node-pause\nntpdir --node-resume\nntpdir --node-status\nntpdir --power restart|shutdown\nntpdir --power restart --in 10\nntpdir --power shutdown --at 02:30\nntpdir --power status\nntpdir --power cancel\n(--yes skips the question and goes ahead even if a gateway would be left unserved)"],
    ],
  },
  stats: {
    title: "Monitor: Statistics",
    body: [
      "How many NTP requests chrony on this node answered, how they ended and who asked. Every ten seconds AnyTime reads chrony's per-client totals (`chronyc clients`) and counts what is new, for every address chrony answers on, and keeps it for **30 days**. A request is counted when the next reading finds it, so the last ten seconds are not in the numbers yet; chrony's own memory for clients is limited, so a very large number of clients can be undercounted. They are held in memory and saved to the node's state directory every 5 minutes and when AnyTime stops, so a restart or an update does not empty them (time AnyTime was not running shows as a gap).",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["See how many requests came in and how they ended.", "Find out which clients ask most.", "Block a client, or look it up, ping it or scan it straight from the list."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: pick a period"],
      ["ol", ["Choose **Now**, **Last Hour**, **Last Day**, **Last Week**, **Last Month** or **Custom** (the **Time range** buttons).", "For **Custom**, set **From** and **To** inside the last 30 days and press **Apply**.", "**Now** is the current 10-minute period so far (5 to 15 minutes). The chart uses one point per minute, 10 minutes, hour or 3 hours, depending on the range."]],
      ["h", "The tiles"],
      ["ul", ["**Total Requests** is everything. **Answered** is a request chrony replied to. **Refused** is a request chrony dropped (over the rate limit). **Failed** and **Kiss-o'-death** stay at 0: chrony does not report them per client.", "**RPS** is requests per second in the last finished interval. The small line gives the highest interval of the range, and the tooltip the average. Click it to draw requests per second in the chart instead of counts.", "**Clients** is the number of different addresses that asked. Click it to draw the clients counted so far. In a cluster a client that asked several nodes counts once. It is not a filter.", "Click a tile to see only that kind of outcome. The chart then draws just that line, and the donuts and the top lists count only those requests. Click **Total Requests** to get everything back."]],
      ["h", "The lists"],
      ["ul", ["The two donuts split the range by outcome (answered, refused and so on) and by IP family. chrony does not report the NTP version a client used, so there is no card for it.", "**Top Clients** lists who asked most. **Top Servers** lists the nodes that answered, by host name.", "Both lists show every result (up to 500 names), ten to a page.", "The **Filter** box shows every entry whose address or name contains what you type, from everything counted in the range. **Esc** empties it. Join words with **AND** or **OR** (AND binds first).", "Click a **client** or the **server** to narrow the other list to it. **Show all** (or clicking the row again) clears it.", "Click a name in the chart's legend to hide or show that line. Hover the chart for the values at one point."]],
      ["h", "Right-click a client or a server"],
      ["ul", ["**Lookup**: what this node's resolver says (the reverse names of an address; the addresses, canonical name, mail and name servers and text records of a name).", "**Whois**: the registry record of its address (network, name, organization, country).", "**Copy**, **Ping** and **Traceroute**: a popup with the output of ping (four packets) or of mtr (ten probes per hop), run on this node. A traceroute has a **Resolve names** box: ticked, each hop shows its host name (looked up by this node as the hop appears) where it has one; unticked, its address. You can switch at any time, and the choice is remembered. **Run again** repeats it. They need the `ping` and `mtr` packages, which the installer adds.", "On a client, **Name** (a name you give a client is shown under its address and found by the Filter box, shared by the cluster) and **Scan** (nmap against the client: the 100 commonest ports, service and OS detection, up to two minutes).", "Hovering a client shows every reverse-DNS name found for it.", "On a client, **Block** and **Unblock** add or remove it on Configure ▸ NTP ▸ Clients ▸ Blocked clients. A blocked client has a 🚫."]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["New clients are noticed every ten minutes for ranges up to a day and a half, and every hour beyond, so the Clients line moves in steps. A slot that hit its client cap makes the number a lower bound.", "Every ten seconds AnyTime reads chrony's per-client totals and counts what is new. The last ten seconds are therefore not in the numbers yet, and chrony's own memory for clients is limited, so a very large number of clients can be undercounted."]],
      ["h", "Clearing the counts"],
      "**Clear**, next to the range buttons, forgets the counts and top lists of this node, or of every node when the Node menu says Cluster.",
      ["h", "In a cluster"],
      "The **Node** menu's last entry, **Cluster**, shows every node's numbers added together. Otherwise the page shows the node picked in the Node menu; each node counts only what it answered itself.",
      ["h", "Memory guard"],
      "Statistics, the host history and the request log live in memory. Every 30 seconds AnyTime checks how much of the machine's memory (or of its container's limit) is in use; at **85%** or more it drops the oldest history — the oldest tenth of what the statistics and the host history cover, per round —, measures again, and repeats (at most 5 rounds per check) until use is below the limit. Newer data always stays, and the newest hour is never dropped. When it has done so the footer of this page says when and what. `NTPDIR_MEMORY_LIMIT_PERCENT` in the daemon's environment changes the limit.",
      ["h", "Limits"],
      "Top lists are collected in 10-minute steps for the last day and in hourly steps for the 30 days, and keep the busiest 300 clients and 600 servers of each step; the rest are counted as “(others)” so a flood of random addresses cannot use up memory. A range longer than a day reads the hourly steps, so its top lists begin and end on a whole hour.",
      ["h", "Fields"],
      ["fields", [["Time range", "The buttons **Last hour**, **Last day** and **Last week** choose how much history the graphs show. They are per window; the history kept is 7 days."], ["From", "With **Custom**: the start of the range, a date and time inside the last 30 days."], ["To", "With **Custom**: the end of the range. Press **Apply** to load it."]]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --stats\nntpdir --stats --all-nodes   (every cluster node's numbers added together)\nntpdir --stats --stats-range now\nntpdir --stats --stats-range 1d   (also 7d, 30d)\nntpdir --stats --stats-outcome failed   (also answered, kod, refused: the lists count only that outcome)\nntpdir --stats --stats-client ADDR   (the servers that answered that client)\nntpdir --stats --stats-server ADDR   (the clients that server served)\nntpdir --stats-clear   (forget the counts)\nntpdir --whois ADDR   (the registry record the Whois popup shows)\nntpdir --host-lookup ADDR|NAME   (its reverse name, or its address)\nntpdir --ping ADDR\nntpdir --traceroute ADDR\nntpdir --scan ADDR   (nmap of a client)"],
    ],
  },
  host: {
    title: "Monitor: Host",
    body: [
      "How busy the machine AnyTime runs on is, on the node picked in the **Node** menu: CPU, memory, disk and network. AnyTime reads the numbers itself from the operating system every 10 seconds and keeps them for **30 days** (per-minute averages, like Statistics). They are saved every 5 minutes and when AnyTime stops, so a restart does not empty them.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["See at a glance how loaded the node is.", "Look back up to 30 days.", "Look at the whole cluster as one machine."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: look at a past period"],
      ["ol", ["Pick a range at the top (**Last Hour** to **Last Month**), or **Custom**.", "For **Custom**, fill in **From** and **To** (inside the last 30 days) and press **Apply**.", "Hover a chart for the value and the peak (the busiest 10-second sample in that point). A gap means the daemon was not running."]],
      ["h", "Reading the page"],
      ["ul", ["The **tiles** are the latest sample: CPU use (and the load average), memory in use (what is not available for programs, caches excluded), the fullest filesystem, how much of the time the busiest disk was busy, and network traffic in and out.", "The **charts** follow the range at the top (Last Hour to Last Month, or a custom start and end). Each point is an average; hover for the value and the peak (the busiest 10-second sample in that point). A gap means the daemon was not running.", "In a cluster, the **Node** menu's last entry, **Cluster**, shows the nodes as one machine: network rates, load averages, memory, swap and disk sizes and cores are **added**; CPU is the average over all the cores (a 12-core node counts three times a 4-core one), memory and each filesystem are per byte of the whole, disk busy is the mean, and a peak is the busiest node's (for the network, the sum of the nodes' peaks, an upper bound). The interface table lists every node's interfaces under its name. A line above the tiles appears only when a node could not answer, and names it.", "**Disk utilization** shows each filesystem's space in use (up to six, the largest first) together with how busy the disks were.", "**Network utilization** adds up the real interfaces. Loopback, ntpdir's own virtual-MAC links (`ntpdir1.1`), container and bridge plumbing (`veth*`, `docker*`, `br-*`, `virbr*`) and ports of a bridge or bond are left out so traffic is not counted twice. The interface table lists them all, except loopback (`lo`), with the link speed the driver reports and each link's use (the busier direction as a share of that speed; “–” when the speed is unknown, as on most virtual machines)."]],
      ["h", "Good to know"],
      ["ul", ["If memory use reaches 85% the **memory guard** drops the oldest history (see Statistics). The footer says so when it has.", "Memory use is the machine's, not ntpdir's. In a container or VM the numbers are what that system reports. Counters that go backwards (a reboot, an interface reset) give a gap instead of a spike."]],
      ["h", "Fields"],
      ["fields", [["Time range", "The buttons **Last hour**, **Last day** and **Last week** choose how much history the graphs show. They are per window; the history kept is 7 days."], ["From", "With **Custom**: the start of the range, a date and time inside the last 30 days."], ["To", "With **Custom**: the end of the range. Press **Apply** to load it."]]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --host\nntpdir --host --all-nodes   (the cluster as one machine)\nntpdir --host --host-range 1d\nntpdir --host --host-range 7d\nntpdir --host --host-range 30d\n(now, average and peak of CPU, memory, disk I/O and network, plus the filesystems and interfaces)"],
    ],
  },
  anycaststatus_bgp: {
    title: "Monitor: Anycast ▸ BGP",
    body: [
      "What BGP is doing on this node right now. This page has two tabs: **BGP** (this one) and **OSPF**. It refreshes every few seconds. The settings are under **Configure ▸ Anycast ▸ BGP**; switching BGP or a neighbor off and on is under **Operate ▸ Anycast ▸ BGP**.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Check that a session to each router is established.", "Check which anycast addresses this node is announcing, and why one is not."]],
      ["h", "Reading the page"],
      ["ul", ["**Status**: the local AS, whether FRR is installed and `bgpd` answers, and what the last apply did.", "**Neighbors**: the BGP session state read from FRR. *Established* is a working session. A neighbor you disabled shows *disabled*. Also shown: the **BFD** state (*up* means the router answers fast failure detection, *down* means it does not), how long the session has been up, and how many prefixes were sent to it.", "**Anycast addresses**: every anycast address of every gateway and whether this node announces it. An address is *withdrawn* while no NTP server answers, the gateway is paused or not running here, or the address could not be added to `lo`. The reason is shown."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: find out why an address is not announced"],
      ["ol", ["Find the address in the **Anycast addresses** list. If it is *withdrawn*, read the reason beside it.", "If the reason is a server or the gateway, look at **Topology** or **Monitor ▸ NTP**.", "If the reason is BGP, look at **Neighbors** for a session that is not *Established*. If you also use OSPF, check its tab: an address is announced while either protocol carries it."]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --bgp\nntpdir --bgp-neighbor-add 192.0.2.1 --remote-as 64500 --description core\nntpdir --bgp-neighbor-add 10.0.1.5 --remote-as 64512 --multihop 2\nntpdir --bgp-neighbor-del 192.0.2.1\nntpdir --bgp-disable\nntpdir --bgp-enable\nntpdir --bgp-neighbor-disable 192.0.2.1\nntpdir --bgp-neighbor-enable 192.0.2.1"],
    ],
  },
  anycaststatus_ospf: {
    title: "Monitor: Anycast ▸ OSPF",
    body: [
      "What OSPF is doing on this node right now. This page has two tabs: **BGP** and **OSPF** (this one). It refreshes every few seconds. The settings are under **Configure ▸ Anycast ▸ OSPF**; switching OSPF or an interface off and on is under **Operate ▸ Anycast ▸ OSPF**.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Check that every interface has a neighbor in state Full.", "Check which anycast addresses this node is announcing, and why one is not."]],
      ["h", "Reading the page"],
      ["ul", ["**Status**: the router ID, whether FRR is installed and `ospfd` and `ospf6d` answer, and what the last apply did.", "**Neighbors**: every interface with its area and, for each neighbor on it, the neighbor's router ID, the protocol (OSPFv2 for IPv4, OSPFv3 for IPv6) and its state. *Full* is a working adjacency; *2-Way*, *ExStart*, *Exchange* and *Loading* are on the way there; an interface with no neighbor shows *no neighbor*, and one you disabled shows *disabled*. Also shown: the **BFD** state (read for OSPFv2 neighbors) and how long the neighbor has been up.", "**Anycast addresses**: every anycast address of every gateway and whether this node announces it. An address is *withdrawn* while no NTP server answers, the gateway is paused or not running here, or the address could not be added to `lo`. The reason is shown."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: find out why an address is not announced"],
      ["ol", ["Find the address in the **Anycast addresses** list. If it is *withdrawn*, read the reason beside it.", "If the reason is a server or the gateway, look at **Topology** or **Monitor ▸ NTP**.", "If the reason is OSPF, look at **Neighbors** for an interface whose neighbor is not *Full*, and check the area, link type, key and timers against the router at the other end. If you also use BGP, check its tab: an address is announced while either protocol carries it."]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --ospf"],
    ],
  },
  capture: {
    title: "Monitor: Capture",
    body: [
      "A packet capture, like `tcpdump`, on one node or on every node of the cluster at once. It only listens: nothing is sent and nothing is changed. It is off until you press Start.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Watch live what a node sends and receives.", "Save the capture as a `.pcap` file for Wireshark.", "Capture on all nodes at the same moment, to see whether what one node sent is what another received."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: capture on one node"],
      ["ol", ["Pick the node in the **Node** menu.", "Choose the **Interface**. A gateway's interface is marked; the virtual-MAC interfaces are there too.", "Optionally type a **Filter** (see below) and press **Start**.", "The newest packets are listed live. Press **Stop** to end it. What was captured stays until you start again.", "**Download .tgz** saves it as an archive with one `.pcap` in it. **Clear** empties the list."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: capture on every node"],
      ["ol", ["Choose **Cluster** in the Node menu.", "Choose the **Interface** (every node must have one of that name) and **How long** (5, 10, 30 or 60 seconds).", "Press **Capture on all nodes**, then **Download .tgz**. You get one `.pcap` per node (this node's is marked `-this-node`), a `summary.txt`, and an `errors.txt` naming any node that could not capture and why."]],
      ["h", "One node"],
      ["ul", ["The capture runs on the node chosen in the **Node** menu. Pick an **interface** (a gateway's interface is marked, and the virtual-MAC interfaces `ntpdirN.M` are there too), optionally a **filter**, and press **Start**.", "The newest packets are listed live, one line each: TCP flags, NTP packets (`NTPv4 client`, `NTPv4 server stratum 2 ref 10.0.0.1`, `kiss-o'-death RATE`), ARP and IPv6 neighbor discovery with the Ethernet addresses they were sent from and to (`ARP, 10.20.0.205 is-at 00:1a:7c:01:02:00 (eth 02:… > …)`), which shows at once whether the virtual MAC a client is told about is the one that answers.", "The buffer holds the newest 5000 packets, or 32 MB. **Download .tgz** saves it as an archive with one `.pcap` in it, for Wireshark or tcpdump (through another node, the newest part that fits about 5 MB). **Clear** empties the list, **Stop** ends the capture (what was captured stays until you start again). A node has one capture: starting another replaces it."]],
      "In the cluster view each node keeps its newest packets (about 4 MB). This does not touch what anyone has on a node's own Capture page.",
      ["h", "The filter"],
      "The filter is a small version of the tcpdump language, applied on the node before a packet is kept: `host 10.0.0.5`, `src host`, `dst host`, `net 10.0.0.0/24`, `port 123`, `src port`, `dst port`, `portrange 50-60`, `tcp`, `udp`, `icmp`, `icmp6`, `arp`, `ip`, `ip6`, `ntp` (port 123), `ether host 00:1a:7c:01:02:00` (also `src`, `dst`), joined with `and`, `or`, `not` and brackets. A protocol before host, net or port narrows it (`tcp port 123`). A mistake is reported when you press Start. Names are not looked up: give addresses. An empty filter keeps everything, which on a busy NTP server fills the buffer in moments, so filter.",
      ["h", "Fields"],
      ["fields", [["Interface", "The network card this node uses for the gateway (default `eth0`). This node only."], ["Filter", "Optional, up to 300 characters, as above. Press Enter to start."], ["How long", "Cluster mode only: 5, 10 (default), 30 or 60 seconds."]]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --capture-interfaces\nntpdir --capture eth0 --capture-seconds 10 --capture-filter \"host 10.20.0.205 and port 123\"\nntpdir --capture eth0 --capture-file /tmp/eth0.pcap\nntpdir --capture eth0 --capture-seconds 30 --all-nodes --capture-file /tmp/cluster.tgz\n(without --capture-file the packets are printed, the first 500; with --all-nodes a file is needed)"],
    ],
  },
  log: {
    title: "Monitor: Log",
    body: [
      "The daemon's log on the node this page is acting on (use the **Node** menu to pick another member). It is the same text that goes to the system journal, and it is also kept in a file in the state directory (`ntpdir.log`, rotated at 100 MB with the two previous files kept), so it survives a restart.",
      ["h", "What you can do here"],
      ["ul", ["Search the log for a word, an address or a name.", "Show only warnings and errors.", "Follow the log live.", "Download what you see, or a full **tshoot** bundle for support."]],
      ["h", "Step by step: find what happened to one server"],
      ["ol", ["Type the address in **Filter text**, for example `10.0.0.5 DOWN`. A line must contain every word.", "Choose a **Time range** and, if you like, a **Level**.", "Tick **Live** to follow new lines as they arrive."]],
      ["h", "Finding things"],
      ["ul", ["**Filter** takes words; a line must contain every one of them (any case); join words with `OR` to accept either, for example `error OR warn` (AND binds first). For example `10.0.0.5 DOWN` or `server 10.0.0.123`.", "**Level** keeps lines at that level and above. Debug lines exist only while the daemon's log level is *debug* (Configure ▸ General).", "**Time range** and **lines** bound the answer; the status line under the list says when older lines were cut.", "**Live** refreshes every few seconds and follows the newest line while you are at the bottom; scroll up to read and it stops following. **Download** saves what is shown as a text file."]],
      ["h", "tshoot"],
      "**tshoot** downloads one .tgz with everything that helps to find out why something does not work, from **every node** of the cluster (the node you are signed in to asks the others, whichever node the Node menu has picked): each node's log (the newest 20000 lines), configuration and its saved versions, cluster view, gateway, NTP-pool, BGP, anycast, virtual-MAC-test and update state, host numbers for the last hour, the addresses, links, routes (every table), rules, neighbors, ARP, listening sockets, the sysctl settings that matter to ARP and routing, the firewall rules, FRR's BGP and BFD output and configuration, service status, the system journal of AnyTime, the kernel log tail and the system's version and load. It always includes 8 seconds of ARP and neighbor-discovery traffic on each gateway interface and a dump of every goroutine of the daemon (what it is busy with, for a node that is up but stuck). Passwords, the gateway key, tokens, join codes and private keys are removed before anything is written, command lines of other programs are not included, and each node's README.txt lists the files and says what could not be collected. A node that cannot be reached has a note instead of a folder. Nothing is changed on any node.",
      ["h", "What is logged"],
      "Server up and down changes, gateway start-up and hand-over, a node's CPU, memory or fullest disk going over 85% (a warning, naming what is over; this node and every cluster member) and the line when it is back at 85% or below, configuration changes (each one says what was enabled, disabled, paused or resumed), updates (including auto-update turned on or off), BGP and web sign-in events.",
      ["h", "Fields"],
      ["fields", [["Filter text", "Words, separated by spaces. A line is shown only if it contains every one, in any case. Applied as you type."], ["Level", "All levels, Info and above, Warnings and errors, or Errors only. Default all."], ["Time range", "The buttons **Last hour**, **Last day** and **Last week** choose how much history the graphs show. They are per window; the history kept is 7 days."], ["Lines", "The most lines shown: 200, 1000 (default) or 5000, newest kept."], ["Live", "Ticked (the default): the list refreshes every few seconds and follows the newest line while you are at the bottom."]]],
      ["cli", "ntpdir --tshoot --all-nodes   (the tshoot bundle of every node, written to ntpdir-tshoot-HOST-TIME.tgz; without --all-nodes just this node; --tshoot-file F.tgz names the file; the ARP / neighbor-discovery capture is always taken)\nkill -USR1 $(pidof ntpdir)   (writes every goroutine's stack to the journal: journalctl -u ntpdir; for a daemon that is up but not answering)\nntpdir --log\nntpdir --log --log-min warn --log-since 6h\nntpdir --log --log-grep \"server 192.0.2.53\" --log-lines 200\n(without --log-lines the whole matching log is printed; redirect it to a file to keep it)"],
    ],
  },
  _default: {
    title: "Help",
    body: ["No help has been written for this page yet."],
  },
};
